WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.161
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4625.

2
00:00:01.161 --> 00:00:02.323
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.323 --> 00:00:02.710
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Okay.

4
00:00:02.710 --> 00:00:03.484
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Y'all ready?

5
00:00:03.484 --> 00:00:03.871
Okay.

6
00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:05.032
<v TANGLAO>Alright you're on.

7
00:00:05.032 --> 00:00:10.452
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright today is Friday June tenth, 2016 and I'm here with the Jourdan sisters.

8
00:00:10.452 --> 00:00:12.000
All sisters, right?

9
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.337
<v EMMA JOURDAN LATOUR>Yes.

10
00:00:12.337 --> 00:00:13.686
<v CAROL JOURDAN PORTER>And a brother's coming.

11
00:00:13.686 --> 00:00:15.372
<v ARSENEAULT>And a brother is coming.

12
00:00:15.372 --> 00:00:17.058
Preston is coming soon after.

13
00:00:17.058 --> 00:00:23.465
And we're here for the Mossville Oral History Project that we're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum.

14
00:00:23.465 --> 00:00:30.209
So for starters . . . And I've never interviewed this many people before so this is going to be new for me.

15
00:00:30.209 --> 00:00:31.558
This should be interesting.

16
00:00:31.558 --> 00:00:36.279
I'll have you start with your full name and then we'll just move down.

17
00:00:36.279 --> 00:00:37.291
<v KATHY JOURDAN JEFFERSON>Kathy Jourdan Jefferson.

18
00:00:37.291 --> 00:00:39.988
<v ARSENEAULT>And Jourdan is . . . Is it with a U?

19
00:00:39.988 --> 00:00:40.326
<v LATOUR>Yes.

20
00:00:40.326 --> 00:00:41.000
<v JEFFERSON>J-O-U-R-D-A-N.

21
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:41.459
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

22
00:00:41.459 --> 00:00:41.919
Okay.

23
00:00:41.919 --> 00:00:42.378
Excellent.

24
00:00:42.378 --> 00:00:43.757
<v LATOUR>The Mossville Jourdans.

25
00:00:43.757 --> 00:00:44.676
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

26
00:00:44.676 --> 00:00:46.054
<v LATOUR>Have a “u”.

27
00:00:46.054 --> 00:00:46.514
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

28
00:00:46.514 --> 00:00:46.973
Excellent.

29
00:00:46.973 --> 00:00:50.649
<v LATOUR>All our other relatives it’s without a “u”.

30
00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.027
<v ARSENEAULT>And . . . Okay excellent.

31
00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:57.081
So there are some people who have the G . . . The J-O-R . . .

32
00:00:57.081 --> 00:00:58.000
<v LATOUR>D-A-N.

33
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:58.357
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

34
00:00:58.357 --> 00:01:00.143
<v LATOUR>The majority of our relatives.

35
00:01:00.143 --> 00:01:02.286
<v PORTER>When our grandfather moved here from . . .

36
00:01:02.286 --> 00:01:03.000
<v CLAUDIA JOURDAN HANDY>Iota [Louisiana].

37
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:03.714
<v LATOUR>From Iota.

38
00:01:03.714 --> 00:01:06.571
<v PORTER>Iota they spelled it a different way here.

39
00:01:06.571 --> 00:01:08.714
<v LATOUR>The school teachers added the “u”.

40
00:01:08.714 --> 00:01:13.000
<v HANDY>So they kind of changed it to Jourdan instead of Jordan.

41
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:13.553
<v ARSENEAULT>Interesting.

42
00:01:13.553 --> 00:01:16.868
Okay we'll get back to that.

43
00:01:16.868 --> 00:01:18.526
So your name?

44
00:01:18.526 --> 00:01:20.184
<v PORTER>Carol J. Porter.

45
00:01:20.184 --> 00:01:24.053
I should have said Carol Jourdan Porter.

46
00:01:24.053 --> 00:01:25.158
<v ARSENEAULT>It's C-A-R . . .

47
00:01:25.158 --> 00:01:25.711
<v PORTER>O-L.

48
00:01:25.711 --> 00:01:26.263
<v ARSENEAULT>O-L-E.

49
00:01:26.263 --> 00:01:27.921
<v PORTER>No not O-L-E.

50
00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.026
Just C-A-R-O-L.

51
00:01:29.026 --> 00:01:30.132
<v ARSENEAULT>Just O-L.

52
00:01:30.132 --> 00:01:32.342
And Porter is P-O . . .

53
00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:34.000
<v PORTER>. . . R -T-E-R.

54
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:34.434
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

55
00:01:34.434 --> 00:01:35.301
<v HANDY>Did you [ . . .?]

56
00:01:35.301 --> 00:01:36.602
<v ARSENEAULT>And your name?

57
00:01:36.602 --> 00:01:37.904
<v HANDY>Claudia Jourdan Handy.

58
00:01:37.904 --> 00:01:39.205
<v ARSENEAULT>And your name?

59
00:01:39.205 --> 00:01:41.807
<v LATOUR>My name is Emma Jourdan LaTour.

60
00:01:41.807 --> 00:01:42.241
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

61
00:01:42.241 --> 00:01:42.675
Excellent.

62
00:01:42.675 --> 00:01:47.880
So talking about the last names again, why did they add a

63
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.313
<c.p>“u”?

64
00:01:48.313 --> 00:01:54.386
<v HANDY>Because the education . . . The teachers here thought it should have a “u” in it,

65
00:01:54.386 --> 00:02:03.494
and they put it in my father's name and we just used it ever . . . The “u” was part of our name.

66
00:02:03.494 --> 00:02:06.096
<v ARSENEAULT>So what was your father's name?

67
00:02:06.096 --> 00:02:06.964
<v HANDY>Shelton Jourdan.

68
00:02:06.964 --> 00:02:07.398
<v PORTER>Senior.

69
00:02:07.398 --> 00:02:07.831
<v HANDY>Senior.

70
00:02:07.831 --> 00:02:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And your mother's name?

71
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.205
<v HANDY>Marian Lemelle Jourdan.

72
00:02:11.205 --> 00:02:13.213
<v ARSENEAULT>And what did they do?

73
00:02:13.213 --> 00:02:16.425
<v JEFFERSON>Father was common labor; my mother did work.

74
00:02:16.425 --> 00:02:24.457
At the beginning she did maid work and at the end she went and got her GED and nurse's assistant.

75
00:02:24.457 --> 00:02:26.465
She was a personal nurse.

76
00:02:26.465 --> 00:02:26.866
<v ARSENEAULT>Grandparents?

77
00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:29.677
What do you remember about your grandparents?

78
00:02:29.677 --> 00:02:31.685
<v PORTER>Grandmother was always at home.

79
00:02:31.685 --> 00:02:34.094
I don't think she ever worked.

80
00:02:34.094 --> 00:02:36.504
<v JEFFERSON>My grandfather was Edward Lemelle Senior.

81
00:02:36.504 --> 00:02:39.315
My grandmother was Ida Mae Towner Lemelle.

82
00:02:39.315 --> 00:02:41.323
My grandfather was a gardener.

83
00:02:41.323 --> 00:02:47.748
He worked for David and [Myre?] Garrison and he worked for the Bells all his life.

84
00:02:47.748 --> 00:02:56.181
My grandmother was a stay at home grandmother and somebody that we could always go to the house and have dinner,

85
00:02:56.181 --> 00:03:01.000
and she babysitted all of us when we were young children.

86
00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.667
<v HANDY>She gave us three hot meals a day.

87
00:03:03.667 --> 00:03:07.000
We would always have a lunch that was . . . she cooked.

88
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:13.333
And what she gave us for lunch wasn't going to be the same thing that she cooked for supper.

89
00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:16.000
It was going to be a different meal.

90
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:22.333
So she taught us how to . . . What I remember most about her, she taught us how to be women.

91
00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:24.667
From very little she'd set the table.

92
00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:26.667
We would eat at the table.

93
00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:33.000
She would make us take an afternoon nap, because that was important because the sun was too hot outside.

94
00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.000
We just had a great grandmother.

95
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.667
She made us who we . . . part of who we are because she taught us how to do so many things.

96
00:03:41.667 --> 00:03:45.667
She didn't have a lot of education, but she was well groomed.

97
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:51.333
<v PORTER>What I remember most about my grandmother, she used to have what she called hair washing day.

98
00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:57.333
And it was about ten girls, our cousins and us, and she caught her water in a system.

99
00:03:57.333 --> 00:03:57.667
<v HANDY>Yeah.

100
00:03:57.667 --> 00:04:02.667
<v PORTER>She'd wash our hair in rain water, because she said it would make it grow,

101
00:04:02.667 --> 00:04:08.333
and we all ended up with plenty of hair so I guess we did make it grow.

102
00:04:08.333 --> 00:04:10.667
But she’d wash one at a time.

103
00:04:10.667 --> 00:04:13.667
She'd comb it out and do us some plats.

104
00:04:13.667 --> 00:04:18.000
When our parents come home, our hair was done for the week.

105
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:26.545
<v JEFFERSON>What I remember mostly about my grandmother is that on holidays, special occasion, it was always table set with her fine china, her tablecloths.

106
00:04:26.545 --> 00:04:29.038
And she always made you feel welcome.

107
00:04:29.038 --> 00:04:31.886
And everything was basically a production with her.

108
00:04:31.886 --> 00:04:37.939
And I think we took that on as part of our personal life because we were taught,

109
00:04:37.939 --> 00:04:45.061
not only from our grandmother, but from our parents that anything worth doing is worth doing it well, or good.

110
00:04:45.061 --> 00:04:53.606
Do it to the best of your ability, and when you do the best of your ability no one can ask for anything else.

111
00:04:53.606 --> 00:04:56.811
That's what I remember being taught by my grandmother.

112
00:04:56.811 --> 00:04:59.659
But my great grandmother, I remember her also.

113
00:04:59.659 --> 00:05:00.371
She lived . . .

114
00:05:00.371 --> 00:05:01.795
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

115
00:05:01.795 --> 00:05:03.220
<v JEFFERSON>Her name was . . . Grandmother . . .

116
00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:03.576
<v PORTER>Ira.

117
00:05:03.576 --> 00:05:03.932
<v HANDY>Towner.

118
00:05:03.932 --> 00:05:05.000
<v PORTER>Ira Towner.

119
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:05.361
<v JEFFERSON>Yes.

120
00:05:05.361 --> 00:05:10.411
She lived across the street from my grandmother and we would go over there.

121
00:05:10.411 --> 00:05:12.215
My grandmother fed us naturally.

122
00:05:12.215 --> 00:05:14.380
My great grandmother fed us spiritually.

123
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:21.234
We would be so excited to go over to Grammy's to listen at her tell us, "Read the Bible."

124
00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:23.038
And we'd discuss Bible stories.

125
00:05:23.038 --> 00:05:32.057
And on Saturday nights she'd come over to our home and spend the night with us and we talked about the Bible all the time.

126
00:05:32.057 --> 00:05:39.633
And our family life was based off of faith and we trusted and believed in God for everything that we had.

127
00:05:39.633 --> 00:05:44.684
And we realized at young children anything God had for you, was for you.

128
00:05:44.684 --> 00:05:52.620
And that’s some of the things that I was taught by my grandparents, and my great grandparents, as well as my parents.

129
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:55.146
<v ARSENEAULT>What church did you attend growing up?

130
00:05:55.146 --> 00:05:56.589
<v PORTER>Willow Spring Baptist Church.

131
00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.032
<v HANDY>It's in DeQuincy [Louisiana]?

132
00:05:58.032 --> 00:06:02.000
<v PORTER>No it's about . . . I guess about twenty miles from here.

133
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.404
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that on High Hope Road?

134
00:06:03.404 --> 00:06:04.340
<v PORTER>It's out that way.

135
00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.277
<v HANDY>But we also attended . . .

136
00:06:05.277 --> 00:06:05.979
<v PORTER>The Methodist Church.

137
00:06:05.979 --> 00:06:06.681
<v HANDY>The Methodist Church.

138
00:06:06.681 --> 00:06:07.851
<v PORTER>When our daddy was sick.

139
00:06:07.851 --> 00:06:08.319
<v HANDY>Was sick.

140
00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:11.362
<v PORTER>And we couldn't go to the Baptist Church we'd go down the street.

141
00:06:11.362 --> 00:06:13.000
<v HANDY>We would walk right to church.

142
00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:13.486
<v PORTER>Yeah.

143
00:06:13.486 --> 00:06:15.429
<v LATOUR>Because Mama didn't drive.

144
00:06:15.429 --> 00:06:15.914
<v HANDY>Yeah.

145
00:06:15.914 --> 00:06:19.314
<v LATOUR>She didn't drive until after Daddy died.

146
00:06:19.314 --> 00:06:22.229
And that was in nineteen . . . Daddy . . .

147
00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:23.200
<v JEFFERSON>Probably nineteen . . .

148
00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:24.657
<v LATOUR>Sixty-four or sixty-five?

149
00:06:24.657 --> 00:06:25.143
<v JEFFERSON>Sixty-six.

150
00:06:25.143 --> 00:06:26.600
<v PORTER>Sixty-six he died.

151
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:30.000
She learned how to drive after.

152
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.691
<v JEFFERSON>His demise.

153
00:06:30.691 --> 00:06:35.180
<v LATOUR>What I remember most about my grandmother is just the feeling of love.

154
00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:38.978
I used to stay all night with her every Friday night.

155
00:06:38.978 --> 00:06:42.086
And my grandfather was Catholic, but she was Baptist.

156
00:06:42.086 --> 00:06:47.612
And my grandfather every Friday he had to have fish for supper because he was Catholic.

157
00:06:47.612 --> 00:06:48.993
She'd cook his fish.

158
00:06:48.993 --> 00:06:52.446
Our . . . We lived just like through a trail from her.

159
00:06:52.446 --> 00:06:56.245
I would go through the trail, stay all night with them,

160
00:06:56.245 --> 00:07:02.115
sometimes I'd sneak back home after I ate my fish and then I'd sneak back over there.

161
00:07:02.115 --> 00:07:04.878
And just being in the bed with her.

162
00:07:04.878 --> 00:07:06.259
Sleeping next to her.

163
00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.331
She was so soft and warm.

164
00:07:08.331 --> 00:07:11.094
And you just felt like it was love.

165
00:07:11.094 --> 00:07:12.820
You know what I'm saying?

166
00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:14.892
I really do, I remember that.

167
00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:17.309
Then one time it was my birthday.

168
00:07:17.309 --> 00:07:19.727
I can't remember how old I was.

169
00:07:19.727 --> 00:07:22.144
She cooked a special dinner for me.

170
00:07:22.144 --> 00:07:22.489
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

171
00:07:22.489 --> 00:07:24.906
<v LATOUR>Field peas and fried chicken and lemonade.

172
00:07:24.906 --> 00:07:26.288
I'll never forget that.

173
00:07:26.288 --> 00:07:31.122
And she set the table and everything and it was just me and her.

174
00:07:31.122 --> 00:07:33.540
That made a big impression on me.

175
00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:36.993
<v HANDY>We talk about her but Pawpaw was . . . He was different.

176
00:07:36.993 --> 00:07:39.065
He act like he didn't love.

177
00:07:39.065 --> 00:07:46.662
He didn't want us there, but when we didn't go he wanted to know, "Why you little childrens not over here today?

178
00:07:46.662 --> 00:07:48.388
I bought all that bread.

179
00:07:48.388 --> 00:07:50.460
Mae made all that blackberry jam."

180
00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:51.842
My grandmother made jam.

181
00:07:51.842 --> 00:07:52.532
She made . . .

182
00:07:52.532 --> 00:07:54.950
<v ARSENEAULT>His bark was worse than his bite.

183
00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:55.295
<v HANDY>Yeah.

184
00:07:55.295 --> 00:07:56.331
She made jam.

185
00:07:56.331 --> 00:07:57.712
She made mayhaw jelly.

186
00:07:57.712 --> 00:07:59.094
She made blackberry jelly.

187
00:07:59.094 --> 00:08:06.000
And he would buy the bread at the bread store, which back then was eight loaves for a dollar.

188
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.085
<v PORTER>Because it was about ten of us at his house every day.

189
00:08:10.085 --> 00:08:12.468
<v HANDY>Yeah because it was nine of us.

190
00:08:12.468 --> 00:08:16.894
So what he wanted . . . he was buying it, but he play like he . . .

191
00:08:16.894 --> 00:08:19.957
<v LATOUR>It was a burden for him to do that.

192
00:08:19.957 --> 00:08:22.681
<v HANDY>For him to do that, but is wasn’t.

193
00:08:22.681 --> 00:08:27.447
And then if you didn't go he wanted to know why . . . you're not there.

194
00:08:27.447 --> 00:08:29.489
And he was a good guy.

195
00:08:29.489 --> 00:08:32.553
He was kind of timid, but he was good.

196
00:08:32.553 --> 00:08:39.021
He always had a large vegetable garden because we got all our vegetables and food from the land basically.

197
00:08:39.021 --> 00:08:42.085
And when we say food, my daddy raised hogs.

198
00:08:42.085 --> 00:08:43.447
My grandfather had pigs.

199
00:08:43.447 --> 00:08:45.830
So that's how we were brought up.

200
00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:48.894
We were raised for doing our own things independently.

201
00:08:48.894 --> 00:08:55.362
Then our grandfather worked for the Garrisons and he taught us a lot of things about gardening because he

202
00:08:55.362 --> 00:08:59.106
<c.p>could take something dead and put . . . bring it back to life.

203
00:08:59.106 --> 00:09:00.809
He just had that gift.

204
00:09:00.809 --> 00:09:03.872
But when I say timid, he could never discipline.

205
00:09:03.872 --> 00:09:05.915
He wasn't the one to discipline.

206
00:09:05.915 --> 00:09:06.596
Our grandmother . . .

207
00:09:06.596 --> 00:09:10.000
<v LATOUR>It hurt him more than it would hurt us.

208
00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:13.441
<v HANDY>Our grandmother had . . . He would go pick the apple switch, but he didn't . . .

209
00:09:13.441 --> 00:09:14.500
<v PORTER>Bring it to her.

210
00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.618
<v HANDY>Yeah, but he wasn't going to use it.

211
00:09:16.618 --> 00:09:19.000
<v PORTER>And my grandmother . . . my grandfather used to . . . Pawpaw Lemelle,

212
00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:22.706
he used to make us toast after he went and bought all his bread.

213
00:09:22.706 --> 00:09:23.765
<v HANDY>All his bread, yeah.

214
00:09:23.765 --> 00:09:27.735
<v PORTER>He'd make us toast and if the toast wasn't right if he didn't burn it.

215
00:09:27.735 --> 00:09:31.176
We had to scrape it before we could put our butter on it.

216
00:09:31.176 --> 00:09:37.000
He always burned it, so we had to scrape it off and then use some of the homemade jelly on the . . .

217
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:42.629
<v HANDY>And I remember he and Big Mama used to be in competition who could make the best biscuits.

218
00:09:42.629 --> 00:09:44.506
<v PORTER>Who could make the best biscuits.

219
00:09:44.506 --> 00:09:44.819
Yeah.

220
00:09:44.819 --> 00:09:50.761
<v HANDY>And we would just have to go with him sometime because we knew she made the better ones but . . .

221
00:09:50.761 --> 00:09:52.950
<v PORTER>Well he did . . . He made the better . . .

222
00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:56.703
<v HANDY>He did cook a little bit better than her in some things,

223
00:09:56.703 --> 00:10:02.332
but the biscuit he would put all that butter in them and the butter on top of them.

224
00:10:02.332 --> 00:10:05.147
And you just loved the way he did it.

225
00:10:05.147 --> 00:10:06.710
But they were great grandparents.

226
00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:07.336
Great grandparents.

227
00:10:07.336 --> 00:10:09.525
And I say that all the time.

228
00:10:09.525 --> 00:10:18.595
If I had to do this all over again I'd want to pass the same way that I came because it taught me how to be a good person.

229
00:10:18.595 --> 00:10:19.846
We were taught morals.

230
00:10:19.846 --> 00:10:24.849
When we were wrong, we were wrong and we had to stand up for being wrong.

231
00:10:24.849 --> 00:10:33.293
So if we had . . . I know from personally if I had to do it again I wouldn't want to leave any of my sisters and brothers out.

232
00:10:33.293 --> 00:10:42.676
When I was younger I used to want to be the only child because I had sisters in front of me and one sister behind me and brothers behind me.

233
00:10:42.676 --> 00:10:46.429
But I realize now that I couldn't have made it by myself.

234
00:10:46.429 --> 00:10:58.000
So I thank God all the time that we were in this group and that the love that we have for each other if we did it again we'd want the same people in our lives.

235
00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.507
<v PORTER>Because she's eleven months older than me [gestures towards Jefferson] and she's eleven months younger than me [gestures towards Handy].

236
00:11:02.507 --> 00:11:03.634
Then there's some in between.

237
00:11:03.634 --> 00:11:03.859
<v LATOUR>Preston.

238
00:11:03.859 --> 00:11:04.535
<v PORTER>Preston is coming.

239
00:11:04.535 --> 00:11:07.014
He's in between [gestures to indicate in between Handy and LaTour].

240
00:11:07.014 --> 00:11:08.141
<v ARSENEAULT>There's five in the family?

241
00:11:08.141 --> 00:11:08.592
Five siblings?

242
00:11:08.592 --> 00:11:09.718
<v PORTER>There's five of us living.

243
00:11:09.718 --> 00:11:10.169
We had . . .

244
00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:11.070
<v LATOUR>No it was nine.

245
00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:11.296
<v HANDY>Nine.

246
00:11:11.296 --> 00:11:12.423
Well actually it was ten.

247
00:11:12.423 --> 00:11:12.873
Mama had . . .

248
00:11:12.873 --> 00:11:14.000
<v LATOUR>She had a miscarriage.

249
00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.743
<v HANDY>And she always said it was five and five.

250
00:11:16.743 --> 00:11:18.571
There was five boys, five girls.

251
00:11:18.571 --> 00:11:25.581
And she always thought it was a reason for us to be so many, and it's to keep taking care of each other.

252
00:11:25.581 --> 00:11:26.800
That was the reason.

253
00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.067
<v PORTER>The only thing just now we all in our sixties almost and we can't . . .

254
00:11:31.067 --> 00:11:35.333
<v LATOUR>Y'all think y'all going to kill me but I got news for y’all [laughs].

255
00:11:35.333 --> 00:11:38.686
<v PORTER>We all of us is in the sixties at the same.

256
00:11:38.686 --> 00:11:39.600
You sixty-five [Jefferson].

257
00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.733
I'm going to be sixty-four this month.

258
00:11:41.733 --> 00:11:42.343
<v HANDY>I'm sixty-two.

259
00:11:42.343 --> 00:11:43.867
<v PORTER>And what are you Emma?

260
00:11:43.867 --> 00:11:44.476
<v LATOUR>I'm fifty-eight.

261
00:11:44.476 --> 00:11:46.000
<v HANDY>And Preston is sixty-one.

262
00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:46.675
<v PORTER>He's sixty-one.

263
00:11:46.675 --> 00:11:53.093
<v JEFFERSON>And what else I remember about my grandparents is my grandfather always had my grandmother a lovely flower garden.

264
00:11:53.093 --> 00:11:53.430
<v HANDY>Yeah.

265
00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:55.457
<v JEFFERSON>And they would pick fresh flowers.

266
00:11:55.457 --> 00:11:58.834
We had fresh flowers in the house at all times.

267
00:11:58.834 --> 00:12:03.225
And I guess that's why I have a love for fresh flowers today.

268
00:12:03.225 --> 00:12:13.020
And my grandmother would arrange flowers if she of learned someone sick in the neighborhood she would arrange flowers and have us go and deliver the flowers to them.

269
00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.397
And she started us in our missionary work as children.

270
00:12:16.397 --> 00:12:21.464
And that's something I always remember, and also that we used to sit on the

271
00:12:21.464 --> 00:12:24.841
<c.p>back porch, my grandfather, and shell peas and shuck corn.

272
00:12:24.841 --> 00:12:27.881
<v LATOUR>In the summer that's how we spent our days.

273
00:12:27.881 --> 00:12:35.311
<v JEFFERSON>And they had fig trees and they would pick the figs, but I was always one that had some kind of excuse.

274
00:12:35.311 --> 00:12:35.987
<v PORTER>Always hiding.

275
00:12:35.987 --> 00:12:37.000
Always hiding.

276
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:39.979
<v JEFFERSON>That I didn't want to do this.

277
00:12:39.979 --> 00:12:42.106
I never really liked doing . . .

278
00:12:42.106 --> 00:12:42.957
<v HANDY>Hard labor.

279
00:12:42.957 --> 00:12:44.660
Let's just say it.

280
00:12:44.660 --> 00:12:46.787
She never liked hard labor.

281
00:12:46.787 --> 00:12:49.340
But we talking about our grandparents,

282
00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:59.979
but one of the things about my daddy I think we should bring in is that they were very Creole when they moved to Mossville.

283
00:12:59.979 --> 00:13:02.532
<v JEFFERSON>And they weren't welcomed very happily.

284
00:13:02.532 --> 00:13:04.234
<v ARSENEAULT>Where were they from?

285
00:13:04.234 --> 00:13:06.362
<v HANDY>They were from Iota [Elton?].

286
00:13:06.362 --> 00:13:07.213
That way.

287
00:13:07.213 --> 00:13:09.766
And he . . . My grandfather, spoke French.

288
00:13:09.766 --> 00:13:11.043
<v PORTER>No another grandfather.

289
00:13:11.043 --> 00:13:13.170
<v LATOUR>Now this is Grandfather Jourdan.

290
00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:17.000
<v HANDY>But anyway . . . Well both grandfathers could speak French.

291
00:13:17.000 --> 00:13:18.107
<v LATOUR>Lemelle and Jourdan.

292
00:13:18.107 --> 00:13:26.227
<v HANDY>But my . . . The thing that I remember most my daddy telling us that they weren't accepted because of their Creole speaking French.

293
00:13:26.227 --> 00:13:29.180
And they would call them the crazy people.

294
00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:30.288
<v JEFFERSON>The crazy Frenchman.

295
00:13:30.288 --> 00:13:34.348
<v HANDY>And they didn't want my mama actually to marry our daddy.

296
00:13:34.348 --> 00:13:37.300
And look what they would have missed [laughs].

297
00:13:37.300 --> 00:13:42.837
<v PORTER>And too because some of us was lighter and some was darker and they thought . . .

298
00:13:42.837 --> 00:13:44.682
<v LATOUR>Well that's a Creole family.

299
00:13:44.682 --> 00:13:46.528
<v HANDY>Yeah they were very Creole.

300
00:13:46.528 --> 00:13:52.433
<v LATOUR>Creole families are . . . the skin color is . . . Can vary from very pale to a rich brown.

301
00:13:52.433 --> 00:14:02.399
<v HANDY>But what I was leading back to my grandparents were that they accepted my daddy and them for who they were because a lot of people said,

302
00:14:02.399 --> 00:14:06.828
"I know you not letting that girl marry that Creole crazy boy."

303
00:14:06.828 --> 00:14:10.150
And my grandmother could see further than they could.

304
00:14:10.150 --> 00:14:19.378
So she was kind of a trailblazer because she accepted them for who they were and not because of what people were saying about them,

305
00:14:19.378 --> 00:14:21.961
or because they spoke a different language.

306
00:14:21.961 --> 00:14:27.129
<v JEFFERSON>And my grandfather that we're speaking of that moved here was Oscar Jourdan, Senior.

307
00:14:27.129 --> 00:14:29.712
And his wife, what was her name?

308
00:14:29.712 --> 00:14:31.189
<v LATOUR>Her name was Ava.

309
00:14:31.189 --> 00:14:32.665
<v JEFFERSON>Ava, that was my . . .

310
00:14:32.665 --> 00:14:33.403
<v LATOUR>Ava White.

311
00:14:33.403 --> 00:14:39.309
<v JEFFERSON>Ava White Jourdan and she died in childbirth so . . . and my father had twelve . . . eleven brothers.

312
00:14:39.309 --> 00:14:41.155
It was twelve boys altogether.

313
00:14:41.155 --> 00:14:43.000
<v PORTER>He had all boys.

314
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:49.364
<v JEFFERSON>All boys and they moved here and now maybe they don't want this in there,

315
00:14:49.364 --> 00:14:59.121
but my grandfather had to ride his horse with a shotgun because the people that was originally here in Mossville they just resented . . .

316
00:14:59.121 --> 00:15:00.394
<v HANDY>To accept it.

317
00:15:00.394 --> 00:15:02.091
<v JEFFERSON>They was like Trump.

318
00:15:02.091 --> 00:15:03.788
They didn't want immigrants.

319
00:15:03.788 --> 00:15:04.636
<v HANDY>In Mossville.

320
00:15:04.636 --> 00:15:08.030
But they persevered because my grandfather was tough.

321
00:15:08.030 --> 00:15:13.970
<v PORTER>Yeah because we end up being one of the most permanent families in Mossville.

322
00:15:13.970 --> 00:15:15.667
<v ARSENEAULT>What brought them to . . .?

323
00:15:15.667 --> 00:15:16.515
<v HANDY>Work probably.

324
00:15:16.515 --> 00:15:18.636
<v JEFFERSON>It was work at the . . .

325
00:15:18.636 --> 00:15:20.333
<v LATOUR>He was a logger.

326
00:15:20.333 --> 00:15:22.455
He would . . . With the trees.

327
00:15:22.455 --> 00:15:25.000
He would . . . With the sawmill.

328
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it the sawmill?

329
00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.750
<v JEFFERSON>Sawmill, that's what I'm fixing to say.

330
00:15:27.750 --> 00:15:28.500
With the sawmill.

331
00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:30.000
<v HANDY>So work brought them here.

332
00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.634
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was he originally first employed?

333
00:15:32.634 --> 00:15:33.951
<v HANDY>I don't know.

334
00:15:33.951 --> 00:15:35.707
<v JEFFERSON>Wasn't it the mines?

335
00:15:35.707 --> 00:15:41.415
<v LATOUR>There was a sawmill here, but I don't know the name of it.

336
00:15:41.415 --> 00:15:46.683
They would actually . . . because my father in law, who is Raymond LaTour,

337
00:15:46.683 --> 00:15:51.951
he told me he worked at one time with my grandfather Oscar.

338
00:15:51.951 --> 00:16:02.049
And they actually would cut the trees down and pull with a team of mules to get the trees to where they were.

339
00:16:02.049 --> 00:16:06.000
And they all were of the same Creole descent.

340
00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:07.317
<v ARSENEAULT>LaTours as well?

341
00:16:07.317 --> 00:16:07.756
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

342
00:16:07.756 --> 00:16:12.146
So they . . . but he did tell me they worked together.

343
00:16:12.146 --> 00:16:16.098
But I don't know the name of the company.

344
00:16:16.098 --> 00:16:22.683
<v JEFFERSON>And our father worked for Doctor Charles Fellows on his ranch, or farm, or whatever.

345
00:16:22.683 --> 00:16:24.000
And my . . .

346
00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.979
<v LATOUR>So we always had plenty.

347
00:16:25.979 --> 00:16:27.958
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that a white doctor?

348
00:16:27.958 --> 00:16:28.354
<v LATOUR>Yes.

349
00:16:28.354 --> 00:16:30.729
<v JEFFERSON>Doctor Charles . . . What was his name?

350
00:16:30.729 --> 00:16:31.917
<v LATOUR>Charles Louis Fellows.

351
00:16:31.917 --> 00:16:33.104
A general practitioner.

352
00:16:33.104 --> 00:16:35.479
He had an office in Maplewood.

353
00:16:35.479 --> 00:16:37.458
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it an integrated office?

354
00:16:37.458 --> 00:16:37.854
<v LATOUR>Yes.

355
00:16:37.854 --> 00:16:38.250
We . . .

356
00:16:38.250 --> 00:16:39.042
<v HANDY>But we . . .

357
00:16:39.042 --> 00:16:40.229
<v LATOUR>It was integrated . . .

358
00:16:40.229 --> 00:16:43.000
<v HANDY>For others, but not for us.

359
00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:44.875
<v LATOUR>Yeah it was . . . No, it was segregated.

360
00:16:44.875 --> 00:16:45.411
It was . . .

361
00:16:45.411 --> 00:16:46.750
<v PORTER>It was they had a . . .

362
00:16:46.750 --> 00:16:49.161
<v LATOUR>They had a black section and a white section.

363
00:16:49.161 --> 00:16:51.839
But when we went after hours and he saw us.

364
00:16:51.839 --> 00:16:52.643
Or before hours.

365
00:16:52.643 --> 00:16:54.518
<v HANDY>Or we would go to his house.

366
00:16:54.518 --> 00:16:57.196
<v LATOUR>Go to his house because Mama worked at his house.

367
00:16:57.196 --> 00:16:58.000
<v HANDY>House also.

368
00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:58.360
<v LATOUR>So . . .

369
00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:03.034
<v HANDY>So we didn't sit there and our daddy . . . He let us recognize that,

370
00:17:03.034 --> 00:17:12.742
but we didn't have to sit and wait in that area of the office because . . . I think one reason was because he worked for them so they . . .

371
00:17:12.742 --> 00:17:17.056
<v LATOUR>The other reason was Daddy probably told them we wasn't sitting there.

372
00:17:17.056 --> 00:17:17.775
<v HANDY>Sitting there.

373
00:17:17.775 --> 00:17:19.573
Yeah because he was very . . .

374
00:17:19.573 --> 00:17:21.371
<v ARSENEAULT>So he had particular view?

375
00:17:21.371 --> 00:17:23.888
<v HANDY>Yes he had his views about that.

376
00:17:23.888 --> 00:17:31.798
Even back then and I think that kind of instilled in all his children because we have big views about that also.

377
00:17:31.798 --> 00:17:36.472
<v LATOUR>But in Mossville back . . . I know we've said a lot about our grandparents,

378
00:17:36.472 --> 00:17:42.944
and you know how some kids they're raised by their grandparents, but we wasn't raised by our grandparents.

379
00:17:42.944 --> 00:17:45.820
Our daddy was the king of our house.

380
00:17:45.820 --> 00:17:46.180
<v JEFFERSON>Yes.

381
00:17:46.180 --> 00:17:49.416
<v LATOUR>He set the rules and you followed his rules.

382
00:17:49.416 --> 00:17:51.573
And Mama and Daddy raised us.

383
00:17:51.573 --> 00:17:53.730
Even after Daddy's death Mama continued.

384
00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.888
She raised all nine of us.

385
00:17:55.888 --> 00:18:00.202
But it go back to taking a village to raise a family.

386
00:18:00.202 --> 00:18:02.000
That's how it was.

387
00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:02.592
<v HANDY>In Mossville.

388
00:18:02.592 --> 00:18:06.439
<v LATOUR>Because we had Big Mama and Pawpaw right there, but Daddy and Mama.

389
00:18:06.439 --> 00:18:12.061
Big Mama might have said something, but if Daddy said “no” she knew you was doing what Daddy said.

390
00:18:12.061 --> 00:18:12.357
<v HANDY>Yeah.

391
00:18:12.357 --> 00:18:16.500
<v LATOUR>So it's not like we didn't have parents who was instrumental in raising us.

392
00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.163
<v HANDY>One time she wanted to whip me for something.

393
00:18:19.163 --> 00:18:23.010
My cousin had pushed me into the tree and cut up my knee.

394
00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:28.929
And she was upset because I had said some mean things and she told me, "I'm going to whip you."

395
00:18:28.929 --> 00:18:33.071
And I ran home because I knew my daddy wasn't putting up with that.

396
00:18:33.071 --> 00:18:35.143
And he sent back and told her.

397
00:18:35.143 --> 00:18:38.102
He said, "I'm the only person going to discipline her.

398
00:18:38.102 --> 00:18:40.765
If she needs a whipping I'll make that decision."

399
00:18:40.765 --> 00:18:44.020
And my grandma say, “Don’t think I'm scared of your daddy."

400
00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:45.796
But I knew she respected him.

401
00:18:45.796 --> 00:18:48.459
She wasn't afraid of him, but she respected him.

402
00:18:48.459 --> 00:18:49.347
So that's different.

403
00:18:49.347 --> 00:18:54.082
And I didn't get that whipping from her, but I’ve got whippings from her but I

404
00:18:54.082 --> 00:18:55.857
<c.p>didn't get that one that day.

405
00:18:55.857 --> 00:18:57.633
<v LATOUR>Because he made his own way.

406
00:18:57.633 --> 00:18:57.929
<v HANDY>Yeah.

407
00:18:57.929 --> 00:18:59.408
<v LATOUR>You know what I'm saying?

408
00:18:59.408 --> 00:19:00.000
<v HANDY>Yeah.

409
00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:00.621
<v LATOUR>He took . . .

410
00:19:00.621 --> 00:19:01.862
<v HANDY>He was very independent.

411
00:19:01.862 --> 00:19:08.069
<v LATOUR>There was plenty of us but he always managed to take care of us and he took us to church.

412
00:19:08.069 --> 00:19:10.241
He was just an all-around good father.

413
00:19:10.241 --> 00:19:11.793
<v JEFFERSON>He took us to church.

414
00:19:11.793 --> 00:19:12.724
<v LATOUR>We prayed every . . .

415
00:19:12.724 --> 00:19:14.897
<v HANDY>We had prayer meetings at our house.

416
00:19:14.897 --> 00:19:15.517
<v LATOUR>That's right.

417
00:19:15.517 --> 00:19:17.690
<v HANDY>We had choir practice at our house.

418
00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:20.483
My mama was over the mission at our house.

419
00:19:20.483 --> 00:19:22.655
So we were pretty grounded in God.

420
00:19:22.655 --> 00:19:27.000
And that's what's . . . I think that's what we got all our morals from.

421
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:28.087
<v PORTER>And you know what?

422
00:19:28.087 --> 00:19:29.990
Our daddy was a hard working person.

423
00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:31.350
<v HANDY>Hard working man yes indeed.

424
00:19:31.350 --> 00:19:34.883
<v PORTER>Because even after he worked his eight hours he'd come he'd raise hogs.

425
00:19:34.883 --> 00:19:35.699
He made bacon.

426
00:19:35.699 --> 00:19:37.058
He had a smoke house.

427
00:19:37.058 --> 00:19:37.602
<v HANDY>Smoke house.

428
00:19:37.602 --> 00:19:37.874
Sausages.

429
00:19:37.874 --> 00:19:39.233
<v PORTER>He would smoke our sausage.

430
00:19:39.233 --> 00:19:41.136
We had plenty, plenty food to eat.

431
00:19:41.136 --> 00:19:42.767
He even had a garden planted.

432
00:19:42.767 --> 00:19:45.485
We even . . . I remember one year we even had peanuts.

433
00:19:45.485 --> 00:19:48.476
We had to pull the peanuts and put them to dry.

434
00:19:48.476 --> 00:19:49.291
<v HANDY>He planted strawberries.

435
00:19:49.291 --> 00:19:49.835
<v PORTER>Just everything.

436
00:19:49.835 --> 00:19:51.466
<v HANDY>Because he exposed us to stuff.

437
00:19:51.466 --> 00:19:54.456
<v JEFFERSON>And he taught us how to work at an early age.

438
00:19:54.456 --> 00:19:55.000
<v HANDY>Yeah.

439
00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:56.556
<v JEFFERSON>And we didn't regret it.

440
00:19:56.556 --> 00:19:58.733
Other kids would be playing for holidays.

441
00:19:58.733 --> 00:20:04.956
We would look for jobs for holidays because we wanted more, and he always taught us don't wait for handouts.

442
00:20:04.956 --> 00:20:05.267
<v HANDY>Yeah.

443
00:20:05.267 --> 00:20:06.511
<v JEFFERSON>You go hand up.

444
00:20:06.511 --> 00:20:07.133
You work.

445
00:20:07.133 --> 00:20:11.178
And everything we had we shared with the neighbors or anybody in need.

446
00:20:11.178 --> 00:20:18.333
He was a person that whatever he had, even if we have to sacrifice to help someone else, that's what he taught us.

447
00:20:18.333 --> 00:20:22.067
And the main thing was be the best of whatever you be.

448
00:20:22.067 --> 00:20:26.111
If you no more than a laborer, you be the best at it.

449
00:20:26.111 --> 00:20:28.911
<v LATOUR>He said, "If you have to dig a ditch.

450
00:20:28.911 --> 00:20:31.711
Dig that ditch to the best of your ability."

451
00:20:31.711 --> 00:20:34.822
<v JEFFERSON>And that's the principles that my work ethics is about.

452
00:20:34.822 --> 00:20:35.756
<v HANDY>Based off of.

453
00:20:35.756 --> 00:20:39.800
<v LATOUR>But he was a firm hand and he had high expectations of us.

454
00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:41.667
<v HANDY>And he didn't take no stuff.

455
00:20:41.667 --> 00:20:42.289
<v LATOUR>That's right.

456
00:20:42.289 --> 00:20:45.089
<v HANDY>When I say that, it was no talk back.

457
00:20:45.089 --> 00:20:51.000
He could look at you and you knew to go in the room and don't come back out.

458
00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:54.421
<v LATOUR>But I don't want to give the impression that he was mean.

459
00:20:54.421 --> 00:20:55.277
He wasn’t mean . . .

460
00:20:55.277 --> 00:20:56.702
<v PORTER>He wasn't a mean man.

461
00:20:56.702 --> 00:20:58.698
<v HANDY>He wasn't mean to us because now . . .

462
00:20:58.698 --> 00:20:59.553
<v PORTER>He was firm.

463
00:20:59.553 --> 00:21:00.409
<v HANDY>He was firm.

464
00:21:00.409 --> 00:21:08.962
And he had to be because when Daddy died he was forty-two years old and he had raised us like he was going to be with us all his life.

465
00:21:08.962 --> 00:21:15.234
So he knew then what he had to do to get us ready for life because my brother Preston that's not here,

466
00:21:15.234 --> 00:21:18.370
he would always . . . And I never understood when we was little.

467
00:21:18.370 --> 00:21:20.651
Because I'd go to the farm with him.

468
00:21:20.651 --> 00:21:22.077
<v LATOUR>He was tough on Preston.

469
00:21:22.077 --> 00:21:24.357
<v HANDY>We would go to the farm with him.

470
00:21:24.357 --> 00:21:28.919
We would ride to the farm with . . . And he'd always tell him, "I'm getting you ready.

471
00:21:28.919 --> 00:21:30.630
You got to be a man."

472
00:21:30.630 --> 00:21:33.196
And he had got the stigma, my little brother,

473
00:21:33.196 --> 00:21:38.328
that he was the man of the house when Daddy was sick because Daddy was sick a lot.

474
00:21:38.328 --> 00:21:42.889
When he wasn't there, he was the man, and he learned that at an early age.

475
00:21:42.889 --> 00:21:45.170
To be strong and to be a man.

476
00:21:45.170 --> 00:21:50.017
And we have no regrets about our childhood, because I tell you I could do it again.

477
00:21:50.017 --> 00:21:54.009
<v LATOUR>And he taught us that whatever you have all your brothers and sisters have.

478
00:21:54.009 --> 00:21:56.574
If one of them needs something you help them.

479
00:21:56.574 --> 00:21:58.000
<v PORTER>If you got it.

480
00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:04.682
<v LATOUR>If you down the street and somebody hit one of them don't you come back home without fighting for your sibling.

481
00:22:04.682 --> 00:22:06.591
You'll get your butt whipped good.

482
00:22:06.591 --> 00:22:10.091
<v HANDY>It was a tough lesson, but you had to do it.

483
00:22:10.091 --> 00:22:11.682
<v ARSENEAULT>And how was he raised?

484
00:22:11.682 --> 00:22:13.909
<v PORTER>But you know what he was raised . . .

485
00:22:13.909 --> 00:22:17.091
<v LATOUR>He was the brother of one of the eleven brothers.

486
00:22:17.091 --> 00:22:19.636
<v JEFFERSON>And he had no mother for a while.

487
00:22:19.636 --> 00:22:19.955
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

488
00:22:19.955 --> 00:22:25.682
<v PORTER>Then one thing made him so stern was because when he moved here people so hard on them.

489
00:22:25.682 --> 00:22:29.182
He wanted us to be able to stand up by ourselves.

490
00:22:29.182 --> 00:22:34.909
<v LATOUR>Because we had that Creole in us and then a lot of the other people were more African.

491
00:22:34.909 --> 00:22:35.864
<v HANDY>Than we were.

492
00:22:35.864 --> 00:22:37.136
<v LATOUR>Then we had to.

493
00:22:37.136 --> 00:22:47.000
And then because if you look at our skin tones [Jefferson] she was always way fairer than the rest and they kind of gave her a hard time at school.

494
00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:48.655
<v HANDY>But she fought her way through.

495
00:22:48.655 --> 00:22:52.793
<v LATOUR>She would fight and then faint and then [Carol D.?] had to finish it. [laughs]

496
00:22:52.793 --> 00:22:53.345
<v JEFFERSON>One time.

497
00:22:53.345 --> 00:22:54.724
<v HANDY>Look let me tell you.

498
00:22:54.724 --> 00:22:57.483
She did . . . The kids would pick at her a lot.

499
00:22:57.483 --> 00:22:59.690
<v LATOUR>And then I'd have to fight for her.

500
00:22:59.690 --> 00:23:01.621
<v HANDY>And we would fight for each other.

501
00:23:01.621 --> 00:23:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What would they say?

502
00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:05.432
<v HANDY>Well to her they would say mean things.

503
00:23:05.432 --> 00:23:06.952
<v JEFFERSON>When I started first grade.

504
00:23:06.952 --> 00:23:07.864
<v HANDY>Don't cry baby.

505
00:23:07.864 --> 00:23:10.904
<v JEFFERSON>The older kids would always say, "Where are you from?

506
00:23:10.904 --> 00:23:12.728
You don't look . . . What are you?"

507
00:23:12.728 --> 00:23:13.640
<v LATOUR>Hello [on phone].

508
00:23:13.640 --> 00:23:15.464
<v PORTER>And she had long ponytails see.

509
00:23:15.464 --> 00:23:18.808
<v JEFFERSON>And so you know one day I made up a story . . .

510
00:23:18.808 --> 00:23:19.112
<v LATOUR>Okay.

511
00:23:19.112 --> 00:23:19.720
Bye bye.

512
00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:20.936
Preston can’t get in.

513
00:23:20.936 --> 00:23:22.456
<v JEFFERSON>I made up a story.

514
00:23:22.456 --> 00:23:28.232
I said my uncle had been in the war and he had women that looked kind of like me.

515
00:23:28.232 --> 00:23:35.224
So I told them my father had me when he was in the military and he brought me back home to my mom . . .

516
00:23:35.224 --> 00:23:35.832
<v HANDY>That’s far-fetched.

517
00:23:35.832 --> 00:23:38.264
<v JEFFERSON>And my mom accepted me as her own.

518
00:23:38.264 --> 00:23:41.000
And they left me alone at that point.

519
00:23:41.000 --> 00:23:43.895
<v HANDY>Yeah but they used to do us some mean things.

520
00:23:43.895 --> 00:23:49.395
<v JEFFERSON>But they was always . . . And we never really fit in because like I say we always was production people.

521
00:23:49.395 --> 00:23:50.263
Whatever you did . . .

522
00:23:50.263 --> 00:23:52.289
<v PORTER>You put a big thing on it.

523
00:23:52.289 --> 00:23:56.342
<v HANDY>And you know one thing I learned the kids always thought we were rich.

524
00:23:56.342 --> 00:23:57.789
<v JEFFERSON>Because we wore nice clothes.

525
00:23:57.789 --> 00:24:00.684
<v HANDY>We went to school with our hair combed every day.

526
00:24:00.684 --> 00:24:03.289
<v PORTER>But they didn’t know where we got those clothes.

527
00:24:03.289 --> 00:24:06.474
<v HANDY>Yeah our mom . . . Yeah because we wore hand-me-downs from other people.

528
00:24:06.474 --> 00:24:07.342
<v PORTER>Doctor Fellows kids.

529
00:24:07.342 --> 00:24:08.500
He had five girls.

530
00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.237
<v HANDY>Doctor Fellows had five girls also.

531
00:24:10.237 --> 00:24:12.263
So we just kind of fit in.

532
00:24:12.263 --> 00:24:14.000
<v LATOUR>This is our brother Preston.

533
00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:14.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Hi Preston.

534
00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:15.500
<v JOURDAN>Hi how you doing?

535
00:24:15.500 --> 00:24:16.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Nice to meet you.

536
00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:17.250
<v JOURDAN>Yeah you too.

537
00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:20.750
<v LATOUR>Oh and the other thing in our family the male is always the leader.

538
00:24:20.750 --> 00:24:22.500
<v HANDY>Yes that's how our daddy taught us.

539
00:24:22.500 --> 00:24:25.000
<v PORTER>He's younger than us but that's how a big brother.

540
00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:29.750
Whatever he tell us to do we fall in line because we was always taught to respect the male.

541
00:24:29.750 --> 00:24:32.250
<v HANDY>The oldest one at home that was the person that . . .

542
00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.000
<v PORTER>This is the oldest girl [indicates Jefferson].

543
00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:35.750
<v HANDY>Well we kind of respect her sometimes.

544
00:24:35.750 --> 00:24:38.000
<v PORTER>The oldest girl but we followed whatever he said.

545
00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:40.000
We respect her, but he the chief.

546
00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:46.420
<v ARSENEAULT>She told us you made up a story that her dad brought her back from . . . She was in the military.

547
00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:46.741
<v JEFFERSON>Korea.

548
00:24:46.741 --> 00:24:47.383
Korean War.

549
00:24:47.383 --> 00:24:48.988
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he in the military?

550
00:24:48.988 --> 00:24:49.309
<v HANDY>No.

551
00:24:49.309 --> 00:24:51.235
<v JEFFERSON>No, but they didn't know that.

552
00:24:51.235 --> 00:24:52.198
<v LATOUR>She just lied.

553
00:24:52.198 --> 00:24:57.012
<v HANDY>She had to get . . . She was tired of being harassed by the kids out there.

554
00:24:57.012 --> 00:25:03.111
So they settled for . . . But they knew who she was because when she would get enough she would put

555
00:25:03.111 --> 00:25:06.000
<c.p>her dukes up and take care of it.

556
00:25:06.000 --> 00:25:15.043
<v PORTER>And you could see them thinking we were different because when you look at us each one of us got something different about us.

557
00:25:15.043 --> 00:25:19.565
That we really don't have the really look of an African American.

558
00:25:19.565 --> 00:25:20.696
We are mixed.

559
00:25:20.696 --> 00:25:21.826
Then also we . . .

560
00:25:21.826 --> 00:25:22.203
<v HANDY>Creole.

561
00:25:22.203 --> 00:25:22.957
Very Creole.

562
00:25:22.957 --> 00:25:29.739
<v PORTER>When we come in, there’s a disease in our family is only . . . It comes from a Mediterranean descent.

563
00:25:29.739 --> 00:25:30.116
<v LATOUR>European.

564
00:25:30.116 --> 00:25:36.145
<v PORTER>So we know it's only . . . We had two brothers and a sister died and our dad.

565
00:25:36.145 --> 00:25:43.681
And it’s only come . . . So we know it's a lot of . . . We had a lot of mixed blood in us.

566
00:25:43.681 --> 00:25:45.565
<v LATOUR>Well I did the DNA.

567
00:25:45.565 --> 00:25:46.696
<v PORTER>Oh you did?

568
00:25:46.696 --> 00:25:54.986
<v LATOUR>Yeah and it's sixty-something African, thirty-five European, fourteen Irish, and then it was just like some little small percentages of everything else.

569
00:25:54.986 --> 00:25:58.000
Oh we . . . one percent American Indian. [laughs]

570
00:25:58.000 --> 00:25:59.832
<v PORTER>Why couldn’t it been more?

571
00:25:59.832 --> 00:26:01.663
<v JEFFERSON>Well that's because so many . . .

572
00:26:01.663 --> 00:26:02.030
<v LATOUR>Generations.

573
00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:04.228
<v JEFFERSON>And our mother was a homemaker.

574
00:26:04.228 --> 00:26:08.990
She taught us how to sew, how to cook, how to clean, and . . .

575
00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:10.089
<v PORTER>How to pray.

576
00:26:10.089 --> 00:26:11.188
A good mama.

577
00:26:11.188 --> 00:26:11.921
Good mom.

578
00:26:11.921 --> 00:26:14.851
<v LATOUR>Yeah Mama . . . We had one of the strongest.

579
00:26:14.851 --> 00:26:16.683
<v HANDY>She was very strong . . . Mamas.

580
00:26:16.683 --> 00:26:18.881
<v LATOUR>Givingest, lovingest mothers there ever was.

581
00:26:18.881 --> 00:26:21.446
She raised us all after Daddy died.

582
00:26:21.446 --> 00:26:22.545
<v HANDY>Like thirty-nine years.

583
00:26:22.545 --> 00:26:24.376
<v LATOUR>She lived to like seventy-five.

584
00:26:24.376 --> 00:26:26.574
We all grew up and married.

585
00:26:26.574 --> 00:26:29.139
She saw a lot of our grandkids.

586
00:26:29.139 --> 00:26:35.000
She helped raise Preston's grandkids and ours, but his was in the house with her.

587
00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:35.662
<v HANDY>His children.

588
00:26:35.662 --> 00:26:36.985
<v LATOUR>His children I mean.

589
00:26:36.985 --> 00:26:39.962
They were in the house with them . . . With her.

590
00:26:39.962 --> 00:26:44.263
And Mama was one of the strongest women you ever want to see.

591
00:26:44.263 --> 00:26:50.218
She went through the loss of her husband and three children and she never wavered in her faith.

592
00:26:50.218 --> 00:26:51.541
She was just tough.

593
00:26:51.541 --> 00:26:56.504
I mean if somebody asked me who my role model is it would be her.

594
00:26:56.504 --> 00:26:57.165
<v PORTER>Mama yeah.

595
00:26:57.165 --> 00:26:59.150
<v LATOUR>Because she was a strong woman.

596
00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:02.789
<v HANDY>And when she had him . . . He was very spoiled by her.

597
00:27:02.789 --> 00:27:04.444
She had him so spoiled.

598
00:27:04.444 --> 00:27:12.383
I remember when he was little Mama would go to work, he'd stand to the fence until she got back so he could nurse.

599
00:27:12.383 --> 00:27:14.038
And was like nine months.

600
00:27:14.038 --> 00:27:15.692
He was a big boy.

601
00:27:15.692 --> 00:27:19.000
<v LATOUR>I didn't realize that until a few years ago.

602
00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:19.921
<v HANDY>That's the truth.

603
00:27:19.921 --> 00:27:22.069
<v PORTER>You don't want to get a whooping [. . .?].

604
00:27:22.069 --> 00:27:23.911
<v JEFFERSON>He wasn’t that big a boy.

605
00:27:23.911 --> 00:27:25.446
He must have been about . . .

606
00:27:25.446 --> 00:27:26.673
<v HANDY>And he was always . . .

607
00:27:26.673 --> 00:27:28.515
<v ARSENEAULT>Still nursing when he had teeth . . .

608
00:27:28.515 --> 00:27:30.970
<v HANDY>Look, he was always bigger than me, okay?

609
00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:33.426
I was the little runt of the bunch.

610
00:27:33.426 --> 00:27:36.802
But he was always bigger, so to me he was big.

611
00:27:36.802 --> 00:27:44.475
And he would . . . even until . . . when Mama . . . Before Mama died when he'd get home from work she had him cookies and milk waiting on him.

612
00:27:44.475 --> 00:27:49.079
And I was a little jealous about that. [laughs] But she had him very spoiled.

613
00:27:49.079 --> 00:27:50.000
<v LATOUR>Or lemonade.

614
00:27:50.000 --> 00:27:52.081
<v HANDY>Yeah she had us all spoiled though.

615
00:27:52.081 --> 00:27:55.054
When it come to her children we were very spoiled.

616
00:27:55.054 --> 00:27:57.432
<v LATOUR>Oh and you didn't mess with her children.

617
00:27:57.432 --> 00:27:59.514
You didn't mess with her grandchildren either.

618
00:27:59.514 --> 00:28:05.459
<v HANDY>No. And if she would call and tell me something about one of the other . . . I said, "Don't call me.

619
00:28:05.459 --> 00:28:06.351
Don't call me."

620
00:28:06.351 --> 00:28:07.541
She’d say . . . I'd say,

621
00:28:07.541 --> 00:28:11.703
"Because Mama you know . . ." In a few minutes she’d say . . . She'd go just opposite.

622
00:28:11.703 --> 00:28:13.486
I'd say, "Mama that's your children.

623
00:28:13.486 --> 00:28:14.378
Leave it alone."

624
00:28:14.378 --> 00:28:17.351
<v JEFFERSON>And she also was adamant about us getting an education.

625
00:28:17.351 --> 00:28:18.541
<v HANDY>Our daddy was, too.

626
00:28:18.541 --> 00:28:20.324
<v JEFFERSON>But my mom was very adamant.

627
00:28:20.324 --> 00:28:22.703
You either . . . You didn't sit around at home.

628
00:28:22.703 --> 00:28:30.730
You either went to work or you went to school and you needed a trade or something because they wanted us to have more than they had.

629
00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:34.000
<v HANDY>Yeah because our daddy had like a fourth grade education.

630
00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:35.628
<v LATOUR>Because each generation should improve.

631
00:28:35.628 --> 00:28:37.256
<v HANDY>But he could build anything.

632
00:28:37.256 --> 00:28:46.372
He didn't need . . . He had that gift that he could take something . . . because every time my mama had a baby he put a new bedroom on the house.

633
00:28:46.372 --> 00:28:48.326
We ended up with about five.

634
00:28:48.326 --> 00:28:48.977
<v PORTER>Five bedrooms.

635
00:28:48.977 --> 00:28:50.279
<v ARSENEAULT>How did they meet?

636
00:28:50.279 --> 00:28:52.558
<v LATOUR>Claudia I thought you knew that story.

637
00:28:52.558 --> 00:28:54.186
She says she saw him.

638
00:28:54.186 --> 00:28:59.395
He was working here at the time they had the air base and he would work.

639
00:28:59.395 --> 00:29:01.349
<v PORTER>Yeah she was working at it.

640
00:29:01.349 --> 00:29:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Chennault?

641
00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:02.342
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

642
00:29:02.342 --> 00:29:03.026
He would . . .

643
00:29:03.026 --> 00:29:05.079
<v PORTER>They had Chennault homes out here.

644
00:29:05.079 --> 00:29:07.474
<v LATOUR>And they had Chennault homes in Maplewood.

645
00:29:07.474 --> 00:29:15.000
And for some reason she’d see him I guess walking to work or something and she fell in love with him.

646
00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:15.964
<v HANDY>And them eyes.

647
00:29:15.964 --> 00:29:17.893
That's what she used to say.

648
00:29:17.893 --> 00:29:19.821
The way his eyes was made.

649
00:29:19.821 --> 00:29:23.036
<v LATOUR>But she actually said too that Clark Gable came here . . .

650
00:29:23.036 --> 00:29:24.000
<v HANDY>And approached her.

651
00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:29.786
<v LATOUR>. . . to make a movie or to visit someone because the Garrisons are very prominent people in Lake Charles.

652
00:29:29.786 --> 00:29:31.393
<v JEFFERSON>It was the Bells, Emma.

653
00:29:31.393 --> 00:29:32.036
<v LATOUR>The Bells.

654
00:29:32.036 --> 00:29:43.286
And she . . . Her dad worked for them and so she was around there because they wanted to adopt her and she said Clark Gable came and wanted to take her back to Hollywood with her.

655
00:29:43.286 --> 00:29:44.893
<v HANDY>To be in the movies.

656
00:29:44.893 --> 00:29:48.429
<v LATOUR>And she wouldn't because she was in love with our daddy.

657
00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:51.000
<v JEFFERSON>And he kind of resembled Clark Gable.

658
00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:51.579
<v LATOUR>He did.

659
00:29:51.579 --> 00:29:55.053
But even I remember as a child she'd call him Clark Gable.

660
00:29:55.053 --> 00:29:55.342
<v HANDY>Yeah.

661
00:29:55.342 --> 00:29:57.368
<v LATOUR>She'd say he looked like Clark Gable.

662
00:29:57.368 --> 00:30:04.895
<v HANDY>And the reason Clark Gable wanted to bring her back was because she didn't look . . . she wasn't . . . She didn't have that look of a black woman.

663
00:30:04.895 --> 00:30:08.947
So she could have been the next . . . The first Lena Horne in the movies.

664
00:30:08.947 --> 00:30:11.263
<v LATOUR>But she decided she loved our daddy more.

665
00:30:11.263 --> 00:30:14.447
<v HANDY>She say she loved Daddy too much to leave Lake Charles.

666
00:30:14.447 --> 00:30:15.895
That's what she actually said.

667
00:30:15.895 --> 00:30:18.789
"I was in love with your daddy I couldn't leave."

668
00:30:18.789 --> 00:30:20.816
<v PORTER>When he died she never married again.

669
00:30:20.816 --> 00:30:21.105
<v HANDY>No.

670
00:30:21.105 --> 00:30:21.974
<v PORTER>She raised us [ . . .?].

671
00:30:21.974 --> 00:30:24.000
<v HANDY>Like thirty-nine years she raised us.

672
00:30:24.000 --> 00:30:27.711
<v JEFFERSON>And she also every time, even after we were married and gone,

673
00:30:27.711 --> 00:30:32.041
she would . . . If you were sick you could count on my mom being there.

674
00:30:32.041 --> 00:30:32.351
<v PORTER>Yeah.

675
00:30:32.351 --> 00:30:32.969
<v HANDY>Yes Jesus.

676
00:30:32.969 --> 00:30:35.134
<v JEFFERSON>Wherever you were she would be there.

677
00:30:35.134 --> 00:30:41.629
And she took care of my daddy for many, many years because he was quite ill for a number of years.

678
00:30:41.629 --> 00:30:44.722
I mean he would never really heal from his illness.

679
00:30:44.722 --> 00:30:45.031
Surgery.

680
00:30:45.031 --> 00:30:47.196
