<v JOURDAN>We was popping firecrackers out there.

901
00:40:31.936 --> 00:40:37.349
Ms. Kellum had to pass us up and go to the store and didn't come back the same way.

902
00:40:37.349 --> 00:40:40.767
So I say, "When she come back go around and get her."

903
00:40:40.767 --> 00:40:44.186
So I had the firecrackers and I had one in my hand.

904
00:40:44.186 --> 00:40:52.163
I had the lighter over here and I got so tickled about it I just got to laughing and then when I looked down I had lit it.

905
00:40:52.163 --> 00:40:57.291
So then the next thing I tried to do was turn it loose, but it was too late.

906
00:40:57.291 --> 00:40:59.000
It popped in my hand.

907
00:40:59.000 --> 00:41:02.137
<v HANDY>He turned around and had to see [about it?]. [laughs]

908
00:41:02.137 --> 00:41:03.706
<v JOURDAN>I say, "Oh Ms. Kellum."

909
00:41:03.706 --> 00:41:05.902
<v HANDY>I said, "Daddy going to get you."

910
00:41:05.902 --> 00:41:07.471
I said, "Where’d Daddy go?"

911
00:41:07.471 --> 00:41:08.725
"Let's not tell him.

912
00:41:08.725 --> 00:41:09.980
Let's not tell him."

913
00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:10.608
We said.

914
00:41:10.608 --> 00:41:14.686
<v JOURDAN>So Ms. Kellum, she said, "No, no. Go home and tell your daddy."

915
00:41:14.686 --> 00:41:15.627
So I waited.

916
00:41:15.627 --> 00:41:18.451
That was probably happened ten o'clock in the morning.

917
00:41:18.451 --> 00:41:22.843
So it was about five o'clock that evening I finally had to give in.

918
00:41:22.843 --> 00:41:24.725
<v HANDY>But you know what I remember?

919
00:41:24.725 --> 00:41:29.745
When you was little we had gone . . . you had a little . . . we had a . . . What's it?

920
00:41:29.745 --> 00:41:33.510
A little dog or something we went in the woods to bury.

921
00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:35.392
<v LATOUR>We buried everything in the woods.

922
00:41:35.392 --> 00:41:36.333
In the trail.

923
00:41:36.333 --> 00:41:37.902
<v HANDY>So those . . . Was it bees?

924
00:41:37.902 --> 00:41:39.784
What had got a hold of?

925
00:41:39.784 --> 00:41:40.098
<v JOURDAN>Wasps.

926
00:41:40.098 --> 00:41:42.922
<v HANDY>Some wasps had got to hold of this baby.

927
00:41:42.922 --> 00:41:47.000
Lord he . . . And we probably wasn't supposed to be where we was.

928
00:41:47.000 --> 00:41:49.368
<v LATOUR>No we always were in the woods.

929
00:41:49.368 --> 00:41:52.412
We’d buried our baby dolls when they got old.

930
00:41:52.412 --> 00:41:54.441
We'd bury birds we had caught.

931
00:41:54.441 --> 00:41:56.132
<v HANDY>And he got stung about . . .

932
00:41:56.132 --> 00:41:57.147
<v LATOUR>We’d bury everything.

933
00:41:57.147 --> 00:41:59.176
<v HANDY>I don’t know about fifteen times.

934
00:41:59.176 --> 00:42:02.897
His head was so . . . And he didn't want to tell Daddy.

935
00:42:02.897 --> 00:42:06.956
And because Daddy was going to bring him to the doctor, okay?

936
00:42:06.956 --> 00:42:08.985
You was going to the doctor.

937
00:42:08.985 --> 00:42:10.000
That's what . . .

938
00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:13.327
<v JOURDAN>I was going to the doctor I would get that whipping.

939
00:42:13.327 --> 00:42:15.141
<v HANDY>Yeah his head was like this.

940
00:42:15.141 --> 00:42:23.005
And then I didn't want him to go to bed that night and something happen to him and we hadn't told Daddy what was going on.

941
00:42:23.005 --> 00:42:28.449
So I had to say, "Daddy did you look at Preston's head lately?" [laughs] So he said, "What?"

942
00:42:28.449 --> 00:42:29.659
He was smart though.

943
00:42:29.659 --> 00:42:37.522
He came in the room and he just was playing with us that night we was going to bed and Preston kept his whole hat on.

944
00:42:37.522 --> 00:42:39.941
And he said, "Boy what happened to you?"

945
00:42:39.941 --> 00:42:41.454
He said, "Don't you know . . .?"

946
00:42:41.454 --> 00:42:42.361
Then we talked.

947
00:42:42.361 --> 00:42:44.176
We got a lesson from him.

948
00:42:44.176 --> 00:42:45.990
<v LATOUR>He could’ve had an allergic reaction.

949
00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:46.898
<v HANDY>He told us.

950
00:42:46.898 --> 00:42:50.527
He said, "You could have died from getting stung so many times.

951
00:42:50.527 --> 00:42:52.946
<c.p>Don't hide that kind of stuff from me."

952
00:42:52.946 --> 00:42:55.063
And so, but it was so cute.

953
00:42:55.063 --> 00:42:56.576
He would always get caught.

954
00:42:56.576 --> 00:42:58.995
Everything he did he was always getting caught.

955
00:42:58.995 --> 00:43:03.229
And he never wanted to tell Daddy and Daddy would always figure it out.

956
00:43:03.229 --> 00:43:05.951
I remember one time he shaved off his eyebrows.

957
00:43:05.951 --> 00:43:09.580
<v PORTER>I think every one of our boys did that at our house.

958
00:43:09.580 --> 00:43:12.000
Maybe not your boys, but mines did.

959
00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:16.510
<v HANDY>And he had his hat on and I think Kenny Lee . . . Kenny Lee told Daddy.

960
00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:18.014
"Did you see Preston's eyebrows?"

961
00:43:18.014 --> 00:43:30.943
And Daddy said . . . He kept that old cowboy hat pulled down and Daddy . . . We was at Doctor Fellows office cleaning the office that morning because we would get up at six o'clock sometimes and go with our daddy to clean the doctor's office.

962
00:43:30.943 --> 00:43:32.446
And he was busy working.

963
00:43:32.446 --> 00:43:38.459
Didn't want Daddy to see and Daddy just took . . . knocked his hat back and boy did we laugh at him.

964
00:43:38.459 --> 00:43:39.963
It was so cute though.

965
00:43:39.963 --> 00:43:46.578
He was . . . This was my best friend growing up and he always took care of me no matter how old I got.

966
00:43:46.578 --> 00:43:49.584
We was just like hey, two peas in a pod.

967
00:43:49.584 --> 00:43:55.899
The boys . . . I remember one time the boys was talking about me in the street and none of it was true.

968
00:43:55.899 --> 00:43:56.801
He handled it.

969
00:43:56.801 --> 00:44:10.932
And I knew then that was my buddy because that's the love we had for each other because no one was going to say something that wasn't right about us and we didn't stand up for our self because our daddy had taught us that from early.

970
00:44:10.932 --> 00:44:14.240
<v PORTER>We even had Earl, one of our cousins lived with us.

971
00:44:14.240 --> 00:44:19.051
His mama went out of town to work and she didn't have nobody to keep him.

972
00:44:19.051 --> 00:44:25.365
So we took . . . My daddy and mama took him in and he lived with us and he was about, what Pres?

973
00:44:25.365 --> 00:44:26.868
Six nine or six six?

974
00:44:26.868 --> 00:44:27.470
<v JOURDAN>Six six.

975
00:44:27.470 --> 00:44:31.378
<v PORTER>And he weighed about . . . At that time probably right at three hundred pounds.

976
00:44:31.378 --> 00:44:36.189
He's a big guy because he played football in Grambling after he graduated from high school.

977
00:44:36.189 --> 00:44:36.791
Richard Lee.

978
00:44:36.791 --> 00:44:37.693
You know Richard?

979
00:44:37.693 --> 00:44:41.000
<v HANDY>And he went to the pros for a little while.

980
00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:44.559
<v PORTER>He was . . . My mama would cook a big pot of oatmeal.

981
00:44:44.559 --> 00:44:45.853
<v JOURDAN>That’s our first cousin.

982
00:44:45.853 --> 00:44:46.176
<v PORTER>Yeah.

983
00:44:46.176 --> 00:44:48.441
We'd all eat our oatmeal that day,

984
00:44:48.441 --> 00:44:56.853
but he didn't eat his until the next morning because she made biscuits the next morning and he wanted to crumble his biscuits in his oatmeal.

985
00:44:56.853 --> 00:44:59.765
So he was . . . But he was a good guy.

986
00:44:59.765 --> 00:45:00.088
<v HANDY>Yeah.

987
00:45:00.088 --> 00:45:02.676
He was just like a brother to us.

988
00:45:02.676 --> 00:45:08.500
<v LATOUR>Now you know we talked about our house which is only like two or three lots from here.

989
00:45:08.500 --> 00:45:11.412
And it started off . . . It was a smaller home,

990
00:45:11.412 --> 00:45:18.206
but as the years went on it was big to us until we all moved out and got our own places.

991
00:45:18.206 --> 00:45:26.294
Then when we came back the other day it didn't look as big as it used to look, but for a child it was big.

992
00:45:26.294 --> 00:45:27.912
<v JEFFERSON>Well we had five bedrooms.

993
00:45:27.912 --> 00:45:28.559
<v HANDY>One bathroom [. . .?].

994
00:45:28.559 --> 00:45:36.324
<v LATOUR>We can't talk about living at our house without talking about how we had the ethics of working without talking Rougeau's grocery next door.

995
00:45:36.324 --> 00:45:36.971
<v HANDY>Next door.

996
00:45:36.971 --> 00:45:37.941
Yeah that's true.

997
00:45:37.941 --> 00:45:44.088
<v LATOUR>Because we all worked at Rougeau's grocery store and we lived in an integrated lifestyle way before integration started.

998
00:45:44.088 --> 00:45:45.382
<v HANDY>Before integration was possible.

999
00:45:45.382 --> 00:45:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The Rougeau's were white?

1000
00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:49.211
<v LATOUR>They were a white family from Eunice, Louisiana.

1001
00:45:49.211 --> 00:45:50.039
<v JEFFERSON>They were Cajun.

1002
00:45:50.039 --> 00:45:52.803
<v HANDY>And we learned how to . . . We didn't even notice color.

1003
00:45:52.803 --> 00:45:54.737
<v LATOUR>We'd go in and out of there.

1004
00:45:54.737 --> 00:45:56.118
We'd stay all night there.

1005
00:45:56.118 --> 00:45:57.500
We'd run the store when

1006
00:45:57.500 --> 00:46:00.816
<c.p>they left and went on fishing trips for days at a time.

1007
00:46:00.816 --> 00:46:05.513
<v PORTER>She could hardly see over the register, but Ms. Rougeau put a stool for her to see.

1008
00:46:05.513 --> 00:46:06.618
<v HANDY>Put a little box.

1009
00:46:06.618 --> 00:46:09.105
That was her first . . . That was your first job?

1010
00:46:09.105 --> 00:46:09.934
<v JEFFERSON>My second job.

1011
00:46:09.934 --> 00:46:12.697
I had a dishwashing job in the neighborhood right here.

1012
00:46:12.697 --> 00:46:13.250
<v HANDY>At [Elaine’s?]?

1013
00:46:13.250 --> 00:46:15.461
<v JEFFERSON>She still owe me money . . . She dead [laughs].

1014
00:46:15.461 --> 00:46:17.395
<v HANDY>I think she owed all of us.

1015
00:46:17.395 --> 00:46:19.605
<v JEFFERSON>No I started working about ten years old.

1016
00:46:19.605 --> 00:46:21.263
<v LATOUR>Rougeau's need to be mentioned though.

1017
00:46:21.263 --> 00:46:21.539
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1018
00:46:21.539 --> 00:46:24.303
<v JEFFERSON>And I used to start waiting on the little kids.

1019
00:46:24.303 --> 00:46:27.895
That's who I waited on first, and then they saw that I was . . .

1020
00:46:27.895 --> 00:46:29.000
<v HANDY>She was capable.

1021
00:46:29.000 --> 00:46:32.814
<v JEFFERSON>And I moved up to everybody and by the time I was thirteen my sister,

1022
00:46:32.814 --> 00:46:37.390
the one that's deceased, Katherine, we would run the store while they would go on a week’s vacation.

1023
00:46:37.390 --> 00:46:42.983
But my dad was home sick so he'd come stay with us so we had an adult in the facility with us.

1024
00:46:42.983 --> 00:46:45.780
And I learned to cut meat with the big meat saw.

1025
00:46:45.780 --> 00:46:51.627
I sliced baloney and weighed it on the scale and I think she let all of us have turns working at her store.

1026
00:46:51.627 --> 00:46:53.915
<v LATOUR>Yeah because I worked there too as a child.

1027
00:46:53.915 --> 00:46:54.932
<v HANDY>She let us all.

1028
00:46:54.932 --> 00:46:56.458
And she was good to us.

1029
00:46:56.458 --> 00:46:56.966
She was . . .

1030
00:46:56.966 --> 00:46:57.983
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

1031
00:46:57.983 --> 00:46:59.000
Her first name?

1032
00:46:59.000 --> 00:46:59.379
<v HANDY>Mabel.

1033
00:46:59.379 --> 00:47:00.136
<v LATOUR>[Bueller?] Rougeau.

1034
00:47:00.136 --> 00:47:02.409
I can't remember his name though.

1035
00:47:02.409 --> 00:47:03.545
<v HANDY>It was George?

1036
00:47:03.545 --> 00:47:03.924
<v JEFFERSON>No.

1037
00:47:03.924 --> 00:47:04.682
<v PORTER>Jim, huh?

1038
00:47:04.682 --> 00:47:06.197
<v HANDY>George was the boy.

1039
00:47:06.197 --> 00:47:08.470
<v JEFFERSON>All I know was Mr. Rougeau.

1040
00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:08.848
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1041
00:47:08.848 --> 00:47:09.985
<v PORTER>Wilson was the . . .

1042
00:47:09.985 --> 00:47:11.500
<v LATOUR>Wilson was the son.

1043
00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:13.773
<v HANDY>He wasn't named after his daddy?

1044
00:47:13.773 --> 00:47:14.152
<v LATOUR>[Disagrees].

1045
00:47:14.152 --> 00:47:15.667
We called him Wilson.

1046
00:47:15.667 --> 00:47:23.242
<v JEFFERSON>And what I remember about our house, my daddy was adding on a room and because I was always so . . .

1047
00:47:23.242 --> 00:47:24.000
<v LATOUR>Particular.

1048
00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:27.103
<v JEFFERSON>He made me come out and hand him nails because . . .

1049
00:47:27.103 --> 00:47:28.345
<v HANDY>I loved doing that.

1050
00:47:28.345 --> 00:47:30.828
<v PORTER>When the boys would pass she'd hide behind . . .

1051
00:47:30.828 --> 00:47:33.621
<v ARSENEAULT>So your father built y'alls house from the ground?

1052
00:47:33.621 --> 00:47:35.793
<v HANDY>Well from those first two little rooms.

1053
00:47:35.793 --> 00:47:37.655
Whatever we had at the beginning.

1054
00:47:37.655 --> 00:47:38.586
But he built . . .

1055
00:47:38.586 --> 00:47:40.759
<v JEFFERSON>He didn’t build the first rooms, baby.

1056
00:47:40.759 --> 00:47:44.793
<v HANDY>The first rooms he didn't build, but everything else he added on basically.

1057
00:47:44.793 --> 00:47:45.103
<v JOURDAN>Right.

1058
00:47:45.103 --> 00:47:45.724
<v HANDY>The bedroom . . .

1059
00:47:45.724 --> 00:47:48.207
<v LATOUR>Went from a two room house to, what?

1060
00:47:48.207 --> 00:47:49.448
Seven, eight, nine, ten . . .

1061
00:47:49.448 --> 00:47:50.379
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it wooden?

1062
00:47:50.379 --> 00:47:51.000
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1063
00:47:51.000 --> 00:47:52.333
<v JOURDAN>On the frame . . . on piers.

1064
00:47:52.333 --> 00:47:52.867
<v ARSENEAULT>On piers.

1065
00:47:52.867 --> 00:47:57.667
<v JEFFERSON>And I was always . . . And we had chickens that he'd make me go pick chickens because I was . . .

1066
00:47:57.667 --> 00:47:59.000
<v HANDY>She didn't like that.

1067
00:47:59.000 --> 00:48:00.553
<v JEFFERSON>That was beneath me I thought.

1068
00:48:00.553 --> 00:48:02.624
<v HANDY>She always wanted to live in New York.

1069
00:48:02.624 --> 00:48:04.435
Let me just put it out there.

1070
00:48:04.435 --> 00:48:05.212
<v LATOUR>And we all . . .

1071
00:48:05.212 --> 00:48:06.765
<v JOURDAN>We raised chickens and hogs and . . .

1072
00:48:06.765 --> 00:48:07.800
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of chickens?

1073
00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:08.318
<v JOURDAN>The white . . .

1074
00:48:08.318 --> 00:48:11.941
<v HANDY>They all taste good when you put them in the pot and cook them.

1075
00:48:11.941 --> 00:48:13.753
<v LATOUR>I don't know what they call them.

1076
00:48:13.753 --> 00:48:15.047
<v JOURDAN>It was the white ones.

1077
00:48:15.047 --> 00:48:16.082
I forgot the name.

1078
00:48:16.082 --> 00:48:16.341
<v LATOUR>Domino?

1079
00:48:16.341 --> 00:48:17.118
Is it domino?

1080
00:48:17.118 --> 00:48:17.894
<v JOURDAN>No, they white.

1081
00:48:17.894 --> 00:48:19.447
<v HANDY>I don't remember, but I know . . .

1082
00:48:19.447 --> 00:48:20.482
<v JEFFERSON>And we had roosters.

1083
00:48:20.482 --> 00:48:21.000
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1084
00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:21.275
<v JOURDAN>Anyway . . .

1085
00:48:21.275 --> 00:48:22.922
<v PORTER>We even had a pet skunk.

1086
00:48:22.922 --> 00:48:23.196
<v JOURDAN>Yeah.

1087
00:48:23.196 --> 00:48:24.294
<v PORTER>A long time ago.

1088
00:48:24.294 --> 00:48:25.118
<v HANDY>A pet skunk?

1089
00:48:25.118 --> 00:48:25.392
<v JOURDAN>Yeah.

1090
00:48:25.392 --> 00:48:27.588
<v PORTER>Them people didn’t even know what skunks was.

1091
00:48:27.588 --> 00:48:29.784
They took the scent thing out of it.

1092
00:48:29.784 --> 00:48:31.431
<v JEFFERSON>And we had a pet coon.

1093
00:48:31.431 --> 00:48:33.078
<v JOURDAN>But we would butcher those chickens.

1094
00:48:33.078 --> 00:48:35.000
<v LATOUR>Now y'all sounding a little hillbilly.

1095
00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:35.986
<v HANDY>Well it's true.

1096
00:48:35.986 --> 00:48:36.973
We were hillbillies.

1097
00:48:36.973 --> 00:48:38.288
<v JOURDAN>We always had animals.

1098
00:48:38.288 --> 00:48:40.918
Daddy worked on the farm and so we . . .

1099
00:48:40.918 --> 00:48:43.548
<v HANDY>And you know who else we didn't mention?

1100
00:48:43.548 --> 00:48:44.205
The Prince.

1101
00:48:44.205 --> 00:48:44.863
Doctor Prince.

1102
00:48:44.863 --> 00:48:46.178
<v JOURDAN>They wasn’t in Mossville.

1103
00:48:46.178 --> 00:48:50.781
<v HANDY>No, but they wasn't in Mossville but they was a part of our life.

1104
00:48:50.781 --> 00:48:53.082
<v JOURDAN>We . . . He raised all kind of animals.

1105
00:48:53.082 --> 00:48:54.726
We always had plenty meat.

1106
00:48:54.726 --> 00:48:57.356
And we would butcher . . . I hated butchering chickens.

1107
00:48:57.356 --> 00:48:59.000
<v LATOUR>Oh you and me.

1108
00:48:59.000 --> 00:49:01.918
<v JOURDAN>Chickens and you would butcher like ten, twelve at a time.

1109
00:49:01.918 --> 00:49:02.980
<v LATOUR>I hate butchering hogs.

1110
00:49:02.980 --> 00:49:05.102
<v JOURDAN>The smell of those chickens was something else.

1111
00:49:05.102 --> 00:49:06.959
<v HANDY>I didn't like the smell at all.

1112
00:49:06.959 --> 00:49:08.551
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you butcher a chicken?

1113
00:49:08.551 --> 00:49:09.612
<v PORTER>You just kill it.

1114
00:49:09.612 --> 00:49:12.000
He said butcher but it's just killing them.

1115
00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:12.273
<v HANDY>You . . .

1116
00:49:12.273 --> 00:49:13.636
<v JOURDAN>Chop his head off and . . .

1117
00:49:13.636 --> 00:49:14.455
<v HANDY>Ring their neck.

1118
00:49:14.455 --> 00:49:16.909
<v LATOUR>Ring their neck and then you chop it off.

1119
00:49:16.909 --> 00:49:18.273
<v PORTER>And then chop it off.

1120
00:49:18.273 --> 00:49:24.273
And then you put it under and old tin tub so when it keep flapping it won’t flap all over the yard.

1121
00:49:24.273 --> 00:49:25.636
<v HANDY>And our daddy killed pigs.

1122
00:49:25.636 --> 00:49:27.000
We watched all that.

1123
00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:30.467
<v LATOUR>Yeah he'd shoot them and then jump in the pen and stab them.

1124
00:49:30.467 --> 00:49:32.600
<v JEFFERSON>You try to shot them in the head.

1125
00:49:32.600 --> 00:49:34.733
<v LATOUR>Then after that I was in the house.

1126
00:49:34.733 --> 00:49:37.667
Then the next day you wake up, you open the refrigerator . . .

1127
00:49:37.667 --> 00:49:39.000
<v PORTER>We had fresh meat.

1128
00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:41.803
<v LATOUR>A hog head in the refrigerator looking at you.

1129
00:49:41.803 --> 00:49:43.672
<v HANDY>Mama had made hog head cheese.

1130
00:49:43.672 --> 00:49:47.410
She made hog head cheese and didn’t we made sausages and bacon.

1131
00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:47.721
<v LATOUR>Boudin.

1132
00:49:47.721 --> 00:49:49.279
<v HANDY>Yeah they did do . . . Cousin . . .

1133
00:49:49.279 --> 00:49:49.590
<v JOURDAN>Crackling.

1134
00:49:49.590 --> 00:49:51.148
<v HANDY>What do you call it?

1135
00:49:51.148 --> 00:49:51.459
From . . .

1136
00:49:51.459 --> 00:49:52.082
<v LATOUR>Aunt [Monia?].

1137
00:49:52.082 --> 00:49:54.885
Aunt [Monia?] used to come and make the boudin.

1138
00:49:54.885 --> 00:49:58.000
<v JEFFERSON>And the cracklings was the best in the world.

1139
00:49:58.000 --> 00:49:58.262
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1140
00:49:58.262 --> 00:49:59.833
And you know what I liked?

1141
00:49:59.833 --> 00:50:01.667
You know what I liked about the . . .

1142
00:50:01.667 --> 00:50:02.452
<v JEFFERSON>They were good.

1143
00:50:02.452 --> 00:50:02.976
<v HANDY>. . . the butchering.

1144
00:50:02.976 --> 00:50:05.595
That little pork meat that they cook off the backbone.

1145
00:50:05.595 --> 00:50:07.690
You know right after you do the pig?

1146
00:50:07.690 --> 00:50:09.000
<v JEFFERSON>The back strap like?

1147
00:50:09.000 --> 00:50:09.250
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1148
00:50:09.250 --> 00:50:10.250
<v LATOUR>It's pork back bone.

1149
00:50:10.250 --> 00:50:13.500
<v HANDY>Yeah and they would cook it and Daddy could cook that so good.

1150
00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:15.500
<v LATOUR>But what I didn't like was the smell.

1151
00:50:15.500 --> 00:50:16.750
It would make me sick.

1152
00:50:16.750 --> 00:50:17.500
<v HANDY>Yeah me too.

1153
00:50:17.500 --> 00:50:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did it smell like?

1154
00:50:19.000 --> 00:50:19.656
<v PORTER>Fresh pork smells.

1155
00:50:19.656 --> 00:50:21.188
<v HANDY>I think our blood pressure was lower.

1156
00:50:21.188 --> 00:50:24.906
<v PORTER>It's not stinky, but it's just a stench you can tell that it's meat that's been cut.

1157
00:50:24.906 --> 00:50:25.562
<v HANDY>It's fresh pork.

1158
00:50:25.562 --> 00:50:26.000
<v PORTER>Yeah.

1159
00:50:26.000 --> 00:50:27.797
<v JOURDAN>Just fresh meat is what it is.

1160
00:50:27.797 --> 00:50:29.081
<v LATOUR>It would make you sick.

1161
00:50:29.081 --> 00:50:30.622
It would make me so sick.

1162
00:50:30.622 --> 00:50:31.649
I'd get a headache.

1163
00:50:31.649 --> 00:50:35.500
<v PORTER>But sometimes you say would make your blood pressure got up but that's not true.

1164
00:50:35.500 --> 00:50:37.041
<v JEFFERSON>That's just an old wives’ tale.

1165
00:50:37.041 --> 00:50:42.176
<v HANDY>But you know what, I . . . The last time I remember Daddy and them butchered we had this little pet cow.

1166
00:50:42.176 --> 00:50:42.946
Y'all don't remember?

1167
00:50:42.946 --> 00:50:43.459
Oh Lord.

1168
00:50:43.459 --> 00:50:44.230
You don't remember?

1169
00:50:44.230 --> 00:50:45.000
<v JOURDAN>[. . .?] our pet.

1170
00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:47.298
<v HANDY>He would . . . Everything was a pet in our yard.

1171
00:50:47.298 --> 00:50:49.340
But baby I could not eat that meat.

1172
00:50:49.340 --> 00:50:50.362
I could not fix . . .

1173
00:50:50.362 --> 00:50:51.128
<v JOURDAN>They wasn't pets.

1174
00:50:51.128 --> 00:50:55.468
<v HANDY>Oh, but when we got home from school and we saw that . . . I still can see it.

1175
00:50:55.468 --> 00:50:56.234
It was terrible.

1176
00:50:56.234 --> 00:50:57.511
<v LATOUR>Did you drink his milk?

1177
00:50:57.511 --> 00:50:58.787
<v HANDY>No. He was too young.

1178
00:50:58.787 --> 00:51:00.830
<v JEFFERSON>One of the best things that I remember . . .

1179
00:51:00.830 --> 00:51:01.851
<v HANDY>You drunk his milk.

1180
00:51:01.851 --> 00:51:04.404
<v LATOUR>I know I drank it that's why I'm asking you.

1181
00:51:04.404 --> 00:51:06.957
<v PORTER>Wait and let me tell this while we’re on it.

1182
00:51:06.957 --> 00:51:09.000
We even would make our own butter.

1183
00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:09.222
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1184
00:51:09.222 --> 00:51:09.444
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1185
00:51:09.444 --> 00:51:11.889
<v PORTER>We’d put the milk and we'd churn it in the jug.

1186
00:51:11.889 --> 00:51:12.556
<v HANDY>On the porch.

1187
00:51:12.556 --> 00:51:13.222
And shake it.

1188
00:51:13.222 --> 00:51:15.000
<v PORTER>When you got through you had butter.

1189
00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:17.000
<v HANDY>We did a lot of stuff.

1190
00:51:17.000 --> 00:51:20.333
<v JOURDAN>Old grandpa used to bring fresh cow milk every morning.

1191
00:51:20.333 --> 00:51:21.333
I remember that.

1192
00:51:21.333 --> 00:51:22.333
<v HANDY>Paw paw Jerry.

1193
00:51:22.333 --> 00:51:26.333
<v PORTER>We had the . . . When I bought it that time it burnt you.

1194
00:51:26.333 --> 00:51:26.667
<v JOURDAN>Yeah.

1195
00:51:26.667 --> 00:51:27.667
<v HANDY>It scald him.

1196
00:51:27.667 --> 00:51:30.667
<v ARSENEAULT>You had to boil it before you drink it?

1197
00:51:30.667 --> 00:51:33.333
<v HANDY>Yeah because she could only drink that kind.

1198
00:51:33.333 --> 00:51:35.667
So my daddy and them just . . . we

1199
00:51:35.667 --> 00:51:39.667
<c.p>would boil and get the stuff . . . The bad bacteria out of it.

1200
00:51:39.667 --> 00:51:41.000
<v LATOUR>The same as pasteurizing.

1201
00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:43.000
<v JOURDAN>Yeah they would boil it anyway.

1202
00:51:43.000 --> 00:51:47.667
<v HANDY>Yeah and she accidently . . . One morning he was playing cowboy and he got scald.

1203
00:51:47.667 --> 00:51:50.333
We had some fun in that old house.

1204
00:51:50.333 --> 00:51:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your dad plant by the moon or the almanac?

1205
00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:54.531
<v PORTER>Probably so.

1206
00:51:54.531 --> 00:51:55.594
He had an almanac.

1207
00:51:55.594 --> 00:51:57.188
<v LATOUR>I know they had an almanac.

1208
00:51:57.188 --> 00:51:57.453
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1209
00:51:57.453 --> 00:51:59.312
<v JOURDAN>Yeah we had big gardens because we . . .

1210
00:51:59.312 --> 00:52:00.375
<v HANDY>You mean Pawpaw Lemelle.

1211
00:52:00.375 --> 00:52:02.234
<v JOURDAN>Pawpaw Lemelle had a big garden here.

1212
00:52:02.234 --> 00:52:04.094
<v PORTER>We would plant up at Doctor Fellows’.

1213
00:52:04.094 --> 00:52:05.688
<v JOURDAN>We planted at the farm, yeah.

1214
00:52:05.688 --> 00:52:07.016
And we had big gardens.

1215
00:52:07.016 --> 00:52:10.469
Matter of fact we didn't buy a whole lot of groceries back then.

1216
00:52:10.469 --> 00:52:11.000
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1217
00:52:11.000 --> 00:52:16.110
<v JOURDAN>I was probably . . . I bet I was nine or ten before I ever went to a real grocery store.

1218
00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:19.607
<v LATOUR>Yeah because the only way I would go would be with Big Mama.

1219
00:52:19.607 --> 00:52:20.952
<v HANDY>Big Mama yeah because she . . .

1220
00:52:20.952 --> 00:52:21.759
<v LATOUR>We didn't go.

1221
00:52:21.759 --> 00:52:27.138
We eat . . . If we didn't get it from our garden or from the yard, meat, we'd get it at Rougeau's.

1222
00:52:27.138 --> 00:52:27.945
<v HANDY>Rougeau's grocery store.

1223
00:52:27.945 --> 00:52:31.710
<v LATOUR>The first time I went to A&amp;P I was probably nine or ten myself.

1224
00:52:31.710 --> 00:52:32.517
With Big Mama.

1225
00:52:32.517 --> 00:52:38.703
<v HANDY>And you know what the . . . Ms. Rougeau and them would provide us with everything that we couldn't grow or raise on the land.

1226
00:52:38.703 --> 00:52:41.124
And they was just good about it because a

1227
00:52:41.124 --> 00:52:47.579
<c.p>lot of times we couldn’t even pay for it, but they had made sure we had it because she was just a good person.

1228
00:52:47.579 --> 00:52:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know how they came to Mossville?

1229
00:52:50.000 --> 00:52:52.343
<v PORTER>They were the only white in our neighborhood.

1230
00:52:52.343 --> 00:52:53.808
<v LATOUR>Yeah that's what I'm saying.

1231
00:52:53.808 --> 00:52:59.667
They must have . . . they came . . . I know somebody said they came in '57 around the time when I was born.

1232
00:52:59.667 --> 00:53:01.131
I remember them saying that.

1233
00:53:01.131 --> 00:53:01.717
But why?

1234
00:53:01.717 --> 00:53:03.475
I guess they bought the store.

1235
00:53:03.475 --> 00:53:05.232
Who had the store before them?

1236
00:53:05.232 --> 00:53:07.283
<v PORTER>I don't know who had the store.

1237
00:53:07.283 --> 00:53:09.040
<v HANDY>Was it a store there before?

1238
00:53:09.040 --> 00:53:10.212
<v JEFFERSON>They moved that there.

1239
00:53:10.212 --> 00:53:11.677
<v LATOUR>They moved that store there?

1240
00:53:11.677 --> 00:53:14.313
<v JEFFERSON>They heard about a community that needed a store.

1241
00:53:14.313 --> 00:53:16.364
<v LATOUR>And she's actually our youngest brother's godmother.

1242
00:53:16.364 --> 00:53:16.949
<v JEFFERSON>Not youngest?

1243
00:53:16.949 --> 00:53:17.242
<v LATOUR>Lester’s.

1244
00:53:17.242 --> 00:53:19.000
<v JEFFERSON>I thought she was Felton’s.

1245
00:53:19.000 --> 00:53:20.179
<v LATOUR>She was Lester’s godmother.

1246
00:53:20.179 --> 00:53:20.768
<v JEFFERSON>Excuse me.

1247
00:53:20.768 --> 00:53:21.357
I'm sorry.

1248
00:53:21.357 --> 00:53:24.599
<v HANDY>Two against one . . . We got her. [laughs] Something she didn't know.

1249
00:53:24.599 --> 00:53:32.261
<v LATOUR>I know it was Lester because she'd bring him them cats and . . . I ain't going to tell y’all on record what [I’d do to them?]. [laughs]

1250
00:53:32.261 --> 00:53:34.913
<v ARSENEAULT>[There was something I was going to say about . . .?]

1251
00:53:34.913 --> 00:53:39.628
<v PORTER>And then by mama working in Doctor Fellows’ house when they would clean out the closet,

1252
00:53:39.628 --> 00:53:47.290
well we’d get all the girls’ clothes and they shoes and we really just . . . we kind of just . . . we thought we had . . . We thought we were . . .

1253
00:53:47.290 --> 00:53:49.353
<v HANDY>All that and a bag of chips.

1254
00:53:49.353 --> 00:53:51.121
<v PORTER>Yeah because we never went without.

1255
00:53:51.121 --> 00:53:54.362
We thought . . . we really thought we was . . . Not to say rich,

1256
00:53:54.362 --> 00:53:57.604
but we was better off than other people in the neighborhood.

1257
00:53:57.604 --> 00:54:02.024
<v JEFFERSON>And really and truly the other kid’s parents, most of them worked at the refineries.

1258
00:54:02.024 --> 00:54:05.560
And they really made more . . . Parents made more money than our family.

1259
00:54:05.560 --> 00:54:11.159
But now as sitting back looking at those kids that came from those families we faired out much better.

1260
00:54:11.159 --> 00:54:12.043
<v JOURDAN>Just as good.

1261
00:54:12.043 --> 00:54:12.928
<v LATOUR>Just as good.

1262
00:54:12.928 --> 00:54:16.758
<v JEFFERSON>As good than some of them did and they had a higher income.

1263
00:54:16.758 --> 00:54:17.937
<v LATOUR>Now she sees it.

1264
00:54:17.937 --> 00:54:20.000
She didn't see it back then.

1265
00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:23.397
<v PORTER>And now Kim's father . . . Her mother is our mama's sister.

1266
00:54:23.397 --> 00:54:29.852
Well our daddy died like the first part of December, and her daddy died at the end of December.

1267
00:54:29.852 --> 00:54:36.306
And it was . . . I think it was three men in the neighborhood . . . It had a Joseph man that died,

1268
00:54:36.306 --> 00:54:38.684
then our daddy, and her daddy died.

1269
00:54:38.684 --> 00:54:39.703
<v HANDY>Bunch of kids.

1270
00:54:39.703 --> 00:54:42.421
<v PORTER>And they all had a bunch of kids.

1271
00:54:42.421 --> 00:54:44.799
Well Kim mama just had like three.

1272
00:54:44.799 --> 00:54:45.478
But her

1273
00:54:45.478 --> 00:54:51.933
<c.p>daddy worked at Cities Service, and when they got their social security what they was getting for one child,

1274
00:54:51.933 --> 00:54:54.990
we was getting it for all of us together.

1275
00:54:54.990 --> 00:54:56.349
<v HANDY>All nine of us.

1276
00:54:56.349 --> 00:54:59.067
<v PORTER>And we were . . . Somehow we made it though.

1277
00:54:59.067 --> 00:55:00.426
<v HANDY>And you know what?

1278
00:55:00.426 --> 00:55:01.785
Why we made it?

1279
00:55:01.785 --> 00:55:08.239
Because our oldest brother went to work for the Bell family that my grandfather had worked for in Connecticut.

1280
00:55:08.239 --> 00:55:17.751
He moved to Connecticut and he graduated from high school one day and I remember us going to the train station telling him bye to go to Connecticut.

1281
00:55:17.751 --> 00:55:24.885
And it was the hardest day of my life because we had always been so close and such a knitted family.

1282
00:55:24.885 --> 00:55:28.962
But he knew he had to do that for us to make . . .

1283
00:55:28.962 --> 00:55:31.000
<v LATOUR>But Daddy was still alive.

1284
00:55:31.000 --> 00:55:37.423
<v HANDY>Yeah Daddy was alive then, but he would . . . Even though Daddy was alive Daddy was still kind of sick and he didn't work.

1285
00:55:37.423 --> 00:55:40.495
He worked, but he couldn't work as hard as he did.

1286
00:55:40.495 --> 00:55:41.333
<v JOURDAN>But we all . . .

1287
00:55:41.333 --> 00:55:42.450
<v LATOUR>But everybody worked yeah.

1288
00:55:42.450 --> 00:55:43.009
<v JOURDAN>Everybody worked.

1289
00:55:43.009 --> 00:55:44.126
<v HANDY>Everybody had a job.

1290
00:55:44.126 --> 00:55:44.405
<v JOURDAN>Right.

1291
00:55:44.405 --> 00:55:50.270
<v LATOUR>And the oldest they would work and take care of the ones . . . If you needed something they’d help you get that.

1292
00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:53.342
And Preston had a job all the summers. [Karrie?] had jobs.

1293
00:55:53.342 --> 00:55:54.180
Everybody had jobs.

1294
00:55:54.180 --> 00:55:56.694
<v HANDY>And you know what I remember about my oldest . . .

1295
00:55:56.694 --> 00:55:58.369
<v LATOUR>I had a job back then.

1296
00:55:58.369 --> 00:56:00.045
<v JEFFERSON>I say I had a job.

1297
00:56:00.045 --> 00:56:02.000
<v LATOUR>Oh yeah you had a job.

1298
00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:05.333
<v HANDY>My oldest siblings, they never felt like it was burden.

1299
00:56:05.333 --> 00:56:06.667
They just did it.

1300
00:56:06.667 --> 00:56:09.000
They did it because that's was love.

1301
00:56:09.000 --> 00:56:16.667
And I remember my sister, my oldest sister [Marie?], we was always sleeping in the bed because there was so many of us.

1302
00:56:16.667 --> 00:56:20.333
We didn't have a bed to say this was our bed.

1303
00:56:20.333 --> 00:56:21.333
It was like . . .

1304
00:56:21.333 --> 00:56:22.000
<v LATOUR>I did.

1305
00:56:22.000 --> 00:56:24.333
<v HANDY>Well when we got old . . . Maybe you.

1306
00:56:24.333 --> 00:56:25.333
<v JOURDAN>[. . .?] that big though.

1307
00:56:25.333 --> 00:56:28.333
<v LATOUR>Wait I was older than y'all . . . Younger than y’all.

1308
00:56:28.333 --> 00:56:33.000
<v HANDY>But anyway, she went to work and she bought us all bunk beds.

1309
00:56:33.000 --> 00:56:33.961
<v JEFFERSON>Her and [Bubba?].

1310
00:56:33.961 --> 00:56:40.369
<v HANDY>They bought bunk beds and that was actually the first time we each one of us had our own beds.

1311
00:56:40.369 --> 00:56:43.252
And we were big kids getting their own bed.

1312
00:56:43.252 --> 00:56:44.534
<v PORTER>[Honey cut?] furniture store . . .

1313
00:56:44.534 --> 00:56:51.583
<v JOURDAN>Now running in the neighborhood was always good because you play and as we got bigger as a young teenager, twelve, thirteen,

1314
00:56:51.583 --> 00:56:58.951
like that . . . you would always . . . When you make the circle in Mossville it was always somebody kin to you on the next corner.

1315
00:56:58.951 --> 00:57:00.553
<v HANDY>On the next corner yeah.

1316
00:57:00.553 --> 00:57:06.000
<v JOURDAN>And you just . . . You went from first cousin to first cousin house most of the time.

1317
00:57:06.000 --> 00:57:10.198
<v HANDY>Yeah that's where we played because our daddy wouldn't let us play at too many people houses.

1318
00:57:10.198 --> 00:57:11.432
<v JOURDAN>But it was always fun.

1319
00:57:11.432 --> 00:57:12.914
<v LATOUR>Yeah we had some good times.

1320
00:57:12.914 --> 00:57:15.630
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did y'all do for fun growing up?

1321
00:57:15.630 --> 00:57:16.864
<v PORTER>We’d sweep the yard. [laughs]

1322
00:57:16.864 --> 00:57:17.852
<v HANDY>We count the cars.

1323
00:57:17.852 --> 00:57:18.840
<v JOURDAN>No, we rode horses.

1324
00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:19.827
We always had horses.

1325
00:57:19.827 --> 00:57:20.815
<v LATOUR>Yeah we had horses.

1326
00:57:20.815 --> 00:57:22.049
<v HANDY>And we'd count the cars.

1327
00:57:22.049 --> 00:57:23.037
We had a game.

1328
00:57:23.037 --> 00:57:26.000
<v LATOUR>Going to the farm with Daddy that was fun to us.

1329
00:57:26.000 --> 00:57:26.389
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1330
00:57:26.389 --> 00:57:28.722
<v LATOUR>We worked but it was fun.

1331
00:57:28.722 --> 00:57:30.278
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was the farm?

1332
00:57:30.278 --> 00:57:32.222
<v LATOUR>On the DeQuincy . . . One seventy-one.

1333
00:57:32.222 --> 00:57:33.000
<v PORTER>Route sixty-six.

1334
00:57:33.000 --> 00:57:37.278
<v LATOUR>They had one on . . . in [Ragley?] and one on Route 66.

1335
00:57:37.278 --> 00:57:38.833
<v ARSENEAULT>And it belonged to . . .?

1336
00:57:38.833 --> 00:57:40.000
<v LATOUR>Doctor Fellows.

1337
00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:47.857
<v HANDY>And you know what our daddy would teach us when we was on the way up there he would make sure . . . He'd make us spell our name.

1338
00:57:47.857 --> 00:57:51.931
We'd go over ABC's and you better knew how to . . . You’d better know that.

1339
00:57:51.931 --> 00:57:55.423
You better know that because that was going to be the test.

1340
00:57:55.423 --> 00:57:56.878
<v LATOUR>But it wasn't just us.

1341
00:57:56.878 --> 00:57:58.624
He had kids from the neighborhood.

1342
00:57:58.624 --> 00:58:01.825
They would come and follow us to wherever we were going.

1343
00:58:01.825 --> 00:58:07.937
<v HANDY>And we would make a bag lunch and when we’d get there . . . Sometimes we'd eat the lunch before we got there.

1344
00:58:07.937 --> 00:58:10.265
But we would stay with him all day.

1345
00:58:10.265 --> 00:58:12.302
<v JEFFERSON>And we went fishing on the farm.

1346
00:58:12.302 --> 00:58:13.175
<v LATOUR>Yeah we'd fish.

1347
00:58:13.175 --> 00:58:14.339
Yeah, we had fun.

1348
00:58:14.339 --> 00:58:17.831
<v HANDY>And then there was that . . . On Friday night the church thing. [E.

1349
00:58:17.831 --> 00:58:18.122
Bird's?].

1350
00:58:18.122 --> 00:58:18.704
You remember?

1351
00:58:18.704 --> 00:58:21.905
<v PORTER>Yes the . . . they call them . . . What they call them back then?

1352
00:58:21.905 --> 00:58:25.979
The nights when we'd go we'd all go together the church would have a . . .

1353
00:58:25.979 --> 00:58:27.434
<v HANDY>A thing at Mr. [E.

1354
00:58:27.434 --> 00:58:27.725
Bird's?].

1355
00:58:27.725 --> 00:58:28.889
<v PORTER>What they called it?

1356
00:58:28.889 --> 00:58:32.963
<v HANDY>It was at Mr. E. Bird's he'd like do . . . He would fry fish dinners.

1357
00:58:32.963 --> 00:58:35.000
It was some kind of supper.

1358
00:58:35.000 --> 00:58:35.870
<v PORTER>They called it . . .

1359
00:58:35.870 --> 00:58:38.478
<v HANDY>Yeah they did call it . . . And he would do . . .

1360
00:58:38.478 --> 00:58:38.768
<v LATOUR>Fellowship.

1361
00:58:38.768 --> 00:58:45.145
<v HANDY>We would do . . . He would do peanuts and you could purchase that for him, but it was a function for a family.

1362
00:58:45.145 --> 00:58:47.464
And we would go to the drive-in movie.

1363
00:58:47.464 --> 00:58:51.232
Our daddy would bring a truck load of us to the drive-in movie.

1364
00:58:51.232 --> 00:58:52.681
<v JEFFERSON>You only paid fifty cents.

1365
00:58:52.681 --> 00:58:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What's the name of the movie theater?

1366
00:58:55.000 --> 00:58:55.576
<v HANDY>What was . . .

1367
00:58:55.576 --> 00:58:57.015
<v JEFFERSON>It was the Midway, huh?

1368
00:58:57.015 --> 00:58:59.893
<v HANDY>Right there on highway . . . It was on Highway 90, huh?

1369
00:58:59.893 --> 00:59:00.756
<v LATOUR>On Highway 90.

1370
00:59:00.756 --> 00:59:05.649
<v HANDY>He would bring us to the movies and it would be everybody because it was fifty cents.

1371
00:59:05.649 --> 00:59:08.239
We'd put chairs on the back of the truck.

1372
00:59:08.239 --> 00:59:13.132
<v LATOUR>And I . . . The summer after he died we all learned how to swim at the Mossville pool.

1373
00:59:13.132 --> 00:59:15.434
We'd swim every day sometimes twice a day.

1374
00:59:15.434 --> 00:59:16.298
Everybody but Claudia.

1375
00:59:16.298 --> 00:59:17.161
<v PORTER>She didn't learn.

1376
00:59:17.161 --> 00:59:18.312
<v LATOUR>She don't like water.

1377
00:59:18.312 --> 00:59:20.902
But we . . . That summer we stayed at the pool.

1378
00:59:20.902 --> 00:59:24.356
<v HANDY>Do you . . . You may not believe this, but we could . . . I could.

1379
00:59:24.356 --> 00:59:26.083
I don't know about the rest.

1380
00:59:26.083 --> 00:59:27.810
I could always hear my daddy.

1381
00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:32.990
When it was time to make a decision about doing right or wrong you could always hear it.

1382
00:59:32.990 --> 00:59:35.005
You knew to do the right thing.

1383
00:59:35.005 --> 00:59:38.746
<v LATOUR>You must have been deaf that day you went to the courthouse. [laughs]

1384
00:59:38.746 --> 00:59:39.322
<v HANDY>What courthouse?

1385
00:59:39.322 --> 00:59:41.049
Oh no I asked to go . . .

1386
00:59:41.049 --> 00:59:43.063
<v LATOUR>You were standing up for a cause.

1387
00:59:43.063 --> 00:59:43.351
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1388
00:59:43.351 --> 00:59:45.078
You see that was a cause.

1389
00:59:45.078 --> 00:59:46.805
That's where I get that from.

1390
00:59:46.805 --> 00:59:47.380
From him.

1391
00:59:47.380 --> 00:59:49.971
But I asked to go to jail that day.

1392
00:59:49.971 --> 00:59:51.698
I didn't go just to go.

1393
00:59:51.698 --> 00:59:54.000
It was a purpose and a reason.

1394
00:59:54.000 --> 00:59:59.112
<v PORTER>They call to go to jail I say I'm going to my house. [All speaking at once in background].

1395
00:59:59.112 --> 01:00:07.721
They had a little protest at the little. . . When Ms. Rougeau and them left another family took the . . . and they said something derogatory to one of the young men in the neighborhood.

1396
01:00:07.721 --> 01:00:19.289
So they do a protest and the police come with a paddy wagon and they asked my mama and my brother could they go to jail and they went to jail because they was fighting for they cause. [All speaking at one time].

1397
01:00:19.289 --> 01:00:21.980
<v LATOUR>That's how you got that I made it for you.

1398
01:00:21.980 --> 01:00:24.132
<v JEFFERSON>We also would go camping on the river.

1399
01:00:24.132 --> 01:00:25.477
<v HANDY>Oh yeah I remember that.

1400
01:00:25.477 --> 01:00:26.015
<v ARSENEAULT>Houston River?

1401
01:00:26.015 --> 01:00:26.284
<v PORTER>Yeah.

1402
01:00:26.284 --> 01:00:29.244
Sometimes the little ones we wouldn't sleep out in the camp.

1403
01:00:29.244 --> 01:00:33.548
They'd open the back of the truck and put our pallet there we'd sleep on it.

1404
01:00:33.548 --> 01:00:36.239
<v HANDY>Y'all didn't tell them about Daddy invented the first Suburban.

1405
01:00:36.239 --> 01:00:37.584
<v LATOUR>You want one of these?

1406
01:00:37.584 --> 01:00:37.853
<v JOURDAN>No.

1407
01:00:37.853 --> 01:00:41.619
<v PORTER>He . . . Whether he had a Suburban or not, it was nine in the truck.

1408
01:00:41.619 --> 01:00:42.964
<v JEFFERSON>I know what you mean.

1409
01:00:42.964 --> 01:00:43.234
Yeah.

1410
01:00:43.234 --> 01:00:45.924
He built a cab on the back of the truck.

1411
01:00:45.924 --> 01:00:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Out of what?

1412
01:00:47.000 --> 01:00:47.767
<v JEFFERSON>Wood or something.

1413
01:00:47.767 --> 01:00:48.533
<v JOURDAN>Wood [and tin?].

1414
01:00:48.533 --> 01:00:49.300
<v LATOUR>His face good.

1415
01:00:49.300 --> 01:00:50.322
That's a good picture.

1416
01:00:50.322 --> 01:00:51.856
<v HANDY>We would push his seat up.

1417
01:00:51.856 --> 01:00:59.522
We had a bench there and then there was benches on the way back for all of us to go as a family to church or wherever we were going.

1418
01:00:59.522 --> 01:01:01.056
<v JEFFERSON>And I would hide every time.

1419
01:01:01.056 --> 01:01:04.889
And then one time there was eight of us in the cab of a truck.

1420
01:01:04.889 --> 01:01:06.933
<v PORTER>It was eight of us plus the driver.

1421
01:01:06.933 --> 01:01:08.211
<v JEFFERSON>I was counting the driver.

1422
01:01:08.211 --> 01:01:10.000
<v PORTER>Oh you was counting the driver?

1423
01:01:10.000 --> 01:01:11.607
<v HANDY>We did some fun things together.

1424
01:01:11.607 --> 01:01:14.018
<v JEFFERSON>And we were just . . . They were just as happy.

1425
01:01:14.018 --> 01:01:15.357
<v HANDY>She wasn't, but we were.

1426
01:01:15.357 --> 01:01:16.161
<v JEFFERSON>We were happy.

1427
01:01:16.161 --> 01:01:17.768
<v HANDY>Yeah we had a good childhood.

1428
01:01:17.768 --> 01:01:19.107
<v PORTER>We had a good family.

1429
01:01:19.107 --> 01:01:22.321
<v ARSENEAULT>What about home remedies like when you got stung by the wasp?

1430
01:01:22.321 --> 01:01:25.000
Did your dad and mom have any kind of . . .?

1431
01:01:25.000 --> 01:01:26.444
<v LATOUR>We went to the doctor.

1432
01:01:26.444 --> 01:01:27.889
<v HANDY>Our daddy didn’t play with . . .

1433
01:01:27.889 --> 01:01:29.333
<v HANDY>He believed in the doctor.

1434
01:01:29.333 --> 01:01:31.067
<v HANDY>Because we had our brother had . . .

1435
01:01:31.067 --> 01:01:32.800
<v JOURDAN>He worked for the doctor so . . .

1436
01:01:32.800 --> 01:01:33.667
<v HANDY>It was free.

1437
01:01:33.667 --> 01:01:36.844
And our brother had been sick and she had spinal meningitis.

1438
01:01:36.844 --> 01:01:37.711
<v JEFFERSON>No not spinal.

1439
01:01:37.711 --> 01:01:38.000
Meningitis.

1440
01:01:38.000 --> 01:01:38.289
<v HANDY>Meningitis.

1441
01:01:38.289 --> 01:01:43.200
And so it was . . . Daddy waited to go to the . . . We had to go to the doctor.

1442
01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:45.222
We had to go to the doctor.

1443
01:01:45.222 --> 01:01:47.244
He did not do remedies on us.

1444
01:01:47.244 --> 01:01:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>He didn't grow any herbs in the garden or anything like that?

1445
01:01:51.000 --> 01:01:51.255
<v HANDY>Not . . .

1446
01:01:51.255 --> 01:01:52.786
<v JEFFERSON>Yeah we did use some things.

1447
01:01:52.786 --> 01:01:54.316
They would put some kind of . . .

1448
01:01:54.316 --> 01:01:55.592
<v PORTER>There weren't no herbs there.

1449
01:01:55.592 --> 01:01:58.398
<v LATOUR>The only thing I remember is tobacco on a wasp sting.

1450
01:01:58.398 --> 01:01:59.163
<v HANDY>Wasp sting, yeah.

1451
01:01:59.163 --> 01:02:03.245
<v JEFFERSON>And then they would also make a chain or something to put around the baby’s neck . . .

1452
01:02:03.245 --> 01:02:04.265
<v HANDY>Baby's neck for teething.

1453
01:02:04.265 --> 01:02:06.561
<v PORTER>Go out in the river and get the tea.

1454
01:02:06.561 --> 01:02:08.092
<v LATOUR>And maybe some Chamomile tea, or . . .

1455
01:02:08.092 --> 01:02:12.429
<v JEFFERSON>This was before when they . . . After later when they came about they had modern things they used.

1456
01:02:12.429 --> 01:02:14.724
But we used to drink some kind of tea.

1457
01:02:14.724 --> 01:02:16.000
<v HANDY>She might remember that.

1458
01:02:16.000 --> 01:02:18.443
<v JEFFERSON>And take castor oil to run the cold out.

1459
01:02:18.443 --> 01:02:19.529
<v PORTER>I loved castor oil.

1460
01:02:19.529 --> 01:02:21.700
<v HANDY>And we used to take the fish stuff.

1461
01:02:21.700 --> 01:02:23.057
<v JEFFERSON>And they would give you . . .

1462
01:02:23.057 --> 01:02:23.871
<v PORTER>That’s that red? [ . . .?]

1463
01:02:23.871 --> 01:02:24.957
<v JOURDAN>No castor oil is . . .

1464
01:02:24.957 --> 01:02:26.043
<v LATOUR>I hated castor oil.

1465
01:02:26.043 --> 01:02:28.486
I used to gag and go throw mine up.

1466
01:02:28.486 --> 01:02:35.000
<v JEFFERSON>And they would also give you say like some whiskey with some warm whiskey and honey for your cough and your sore throat.

1467
01:02:35.000 --> 01:02:37.971
<v HANDY>And we would . . . Big Mama used to . . . What was that fish stuff we

1468
01:02:37.971 --> 01:02:38.657
<c.p>used to take?

1469
01:02:38.657 --> 01:02:39.114
<v PORTER>That’s Scotch [?].

1470
01:02:39.114 --> 01:02:40.029
We used to take . . .

1471
01:02:40.029 --> 01:02:40.714
<v ARSENEAULT>What's it called?

1472
01:02:40.714 --> 01:02:41.171
<v PORTER>Scotch and [?].

1473
01:02:41.171 --> 01:02:42.314
<v HANDY>It was a fish oil.

1474
01:02:42.314 --> 01:02:42.543
<v PORTER>Oil.

1475
01:02:42.543 --> 01:02:44.143
It was a bottle about that tall.

1476
01:02:44.143 --> 01:02:45.514
They had a fisherman on it.

1477
01:02:45.514 --> 01:02:46.886
<v HANDY>She'd give you that every morning.

1478
01:02:46.886 --> 01:02:47.571
<v PORTER>I loved it.

1479
01:02:47.571 --> 01:02:48.257
I loved it.

1480
01:02:48.257 --> 01:02:49.171
<v HANDY>Oh it was horrible.

1481
01:02:49.171 --> 01:02:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of tea would they make?

1482
01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:52.140
<v JEFFERSON>They would make tea with . . .

1483
01:02:52.140 --> 01:02:53.053
<v HANDY>They had a tea.

1484
01:02:53.053 --> 01:02:54.193
It was a bitter tea.

1485
01:02:54.193 --> 01:02:55.789
<v JOURDAN>They had a tea Big Mama made . . .

1486
01:02:55.789 --> 01:02:57.614
<v PORTER>They would get out the trees the stuff.

1487
01:02:57.614 --> 01:02:59.895
<v JOURDAN>No the one they used to grow in the herbs.

1488
01:02:59.895 --> 01:03:02.632
<v LATOUR>They make one out of the moss side of the trees too.

1489
01:03:02.632 --> 01:03:03.772
<v JOURDAN>Yeah but I don't know . . .

1490
01:03:03.772 --> 01:03:05.596
<v HANDY>I'm trying to think of the one that . . .

1491
01:03:05.596 --> 01:03:07.421
<v LATOUR>I don't know what that one was called.

1492
01:03:07.421 --> 01:03:08.561
<v HANDY>Oh but it was bitter.

1493
01:03:08.561 --> 01:03:14.719
<v JEFFERSON>They would give you honey and maybe a couple drops of whiskey and something for the cough and they would use this [tally?] or something they used . . .

1494
01:03:14.719 --> 01:03:14.947
<v PORTER>[Tally?] . . .

1495
01:03:14.947 --> 01:03:16.088
<v JEFFERSON>On your chest for colds.

1496
01:03:16.088 --> 01:03:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is tally?

1497
01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:18.253
<v JEFFERSON>It was some kind . . .

1498
01:03:18.253 --> 01:03:18.879
<v JOURDAN>Sheep fat.

1499
01:03:18.879 --> 01:03:19.505
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh okay.

1500
01:03:19.505 --> 01:03:20.758
<v PORTER>They cooked the fat.

1501
01:03:20.758 --> 01:03:30.152
<v HANDY>And our grandfather if you stuck a nail in your foot he'd put a hot [tally?] on it and then put a hot iron on it . . . A knife on it.

1502
01:03:30.152 --> 01:03:33.283
For the . . . You didn't want to stick your [ . . .?] in there.

1503
01:03:33.283 --> 01:03:35.475
You just didn’t want to do that.

1504
01:03:35.475 --> 01:03:35.788
<v PORTER>Okay.

1505
01:03:35.788 --> 01:03:39.232
Do you have any specific questions you need to ask us?

1506
01:03:39.232 --> 01:03:40.172
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah let's see . . .

1507
01:03:40.172 --> 01:03:42.364
<v PORTER>Because we done went over our childhood.

1508
01:03:42.364 --> 01:03:44.556
<v ARSENEAULT>About Mossville, what were the geographic boundaries?

1509
01:03:44.556 --> 01:03:48.000
Like how did you know when you were leaving Mossville?

1510
01:03:48.000 --> 01:03:48.413
<v PORTER>Okay.

1511
01:03:48.413 --> 01:03:52.957
The railroad track on highway . . . this highway . . . on Prater, the highway . . .

1512
01:03:52.957 --> 01:03:54.609
<v JOURDAN>The highway 90 was . . .

1513
01:03:54.609 --> 01:03:56.674
<v PORTER>You were out of Mossville.

1514
01:03:56.674 --> 01:03:58.739
<v JOURDAN>You were out of Mossville.

1515
01:03:58.739 --> 01:04:01.217
Going west to Sulphur you just . . .

1516
01:04:01.217 --> 01:04:03.696
<v PORTER>Got Post Oak Road there on . . .

1517
01:04:03.696 --> 01:04:04.935
<v LATOUR>No before that.

1518
01:04:04.935 --> 01:04:07.000
<v HANDY>By Mount Zion Church.

1519
01:04:07.000 --> 01:04:07.404
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1520
01:04:07.404 --> 01:04:09.426
<v JOURDAN>About a mile past that.

1521
01:04:09.426 --> 01:04:11.851
<v LATOUR>By Ms. LaTour and them house.

1522
01:04:11.851 --> 01:04:13.872
The last house in Mossville.

1523
01:04:13.872 --> 01:04:17.106
<v JOURDAN>LaTour and them was the last black house.

1524
01:04:17.106 --> 01:04:22.766
<v PORTER>And the school was the one from . . . the boundary going toward Westlake. [phone ringing]

1525
01:04:22.766 --> 01:04:24.787
<v HANDY>No, no, no. Further down.

1526
01:04:24.787 --> 01:04:26.000
<v LATOUR>VCM Road.

1527
01:04:26.000 --> 01:04:29.318
<v JEFFERSON>Well we were children we really didn't go there.

1528
01:04:29.318 --> 01:04:30.793
<v HANDY>We didn’t go to . . .

1529
01:04:30.793 --> 01:04:35.955
<v LATOUR>But I'll tell you what my perception of Mossville was Prater Road on to . . .

1530
01:04:35.955 --> 01:04:37.061
<v JEFFERSON>Old Spanish Trail.

1531
01:04:37.061 --> 01:04:42.223
<v LATOUR>Old Spanish Trail and then so far down Old Spanish Trail until the end.

1532
01:04:42.223 --> 01:04:44.804
Then Bel Air was over toward Westlake.

1533
01:04:44.804 --> 01:04:46.279
<v HANDY>That was another area.

1534
01:04:46.279 --> 01:04:48.492
We always thought that wasn't Mossville.

1535
01:04:48.492 --> 01:04:50.704
<v LATOUR>I didn't really consider that Mossville.

1536
01:04:50.704 --> 01:04:52.547
<v PORTER>Because this is really Mossville.

1537
01:04:52.547 --> 01:04:54.760
That was just our little place.

1538
01:04:54.760 --> 01:04:55.497
Our community.

1539
01:04:55.497 --> 01:04:58.078
<v JEFFERSON>They came way after we were here.

1540
01:04:58.078 --> 01:05:02.134
<v HANDY>Mossville . . . Because what I remember most about Mossville is being independent.

1541
01:05:02.134 --> 01:05:06.559
We didn't have any government, or no agencies, no councilmens, no anything.

1542
01:05:06.559 --> 01:05:15.039
We just did our own thing and was able to survive without having it depend on people to make to decisions for us.

1543
01:05:15.039 --> 01:05:18.726
<v JEFFERSON>The only thing we had was the police juryman, huh?

1544
01:05:18.726 --> 01:05:19.464
<v HANDY>Yeah whatever.

1545
01:05:19.464 --> 01:05:23.151
<v PORTER>I don't even remember if we had that back then.

1546
01:05:23.151 --> 01:05:24.257
I don’t remember.

1547
01:05:24.257 --> 01:05:26.101
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were the community leaders?

1548
01:05:26.101 --> 01:05:28.682
<v LATOUR>Yeah we always had a police juror.

1549
01:05:28.682 --> 01:05:29.788
<v PORTER>Mr. Jim [Johnson?].

1550
01:05:29.788 --> 01:05:30.525
<v JEFFERSON>And Rigmaiden.

1551
01:05:30.525 --> 01:05:32.000
<v LATOUR>[Farbacher?] in Westlake.

1552
01:05:32.000 --> 01:05:33.167
<v HANDY>Mr. Jim Johnson was the . . .

1553
01:05:33.167 --> 01:05:34.567
<v PORTER>Mr. Jim Johnson was the leader.

1554
01:05:34.567 --> 01:05:36.200
She said the leaders in the community.

1555
01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:37.133
<v LATOUR>He was a mason.

1556
01:05:37.133 --> 01:05:37.833
<v PORTER>Mr. Jim Johnson.

1557
01:05:37.833 --> 01:05:39.000
<v LATOUR>He was a mason.

1558
01:05:39.000 --> 01:05:40.743
<v JEFFERSON>Jake Rigmaiden back long before . . .

1559
01:05:40.743 --> 01:05:41.440
<v PORTER>Who else?

1560
01:05:41.440 --> 01:05:45.972
<v ARSENEAULT>What did they do that y'all knew that they were the community leaders?

1561
01:05:45.972 --> 01:05:47.716
<v LATOUR>We had the Masonic hall.

1562
01:05:47.716 --> 01:05:51.899
It was a hall and Mr. Johnson would . . . He worked at Olin.

1563
01:05:51.899 --> 01:05:55.385
And he would actually help people get jobs at Olin.

1564
01:05:55.385 --> 01:06:01.312
He actually would have events at the Masonic hall for kids and like for holidays and stuff.

1565
01:06:01.312 --> 01:06:05.147
They would see to it that we the people got stuff.

1566
01:06:05.147 --> 01:06:07.239
Got groceries and toys and stuff.

1567
01:06:07.239 --> 01:06:08.982
<v JEFFERSON>Well I remember Mr. Rigmaiden.

1568
01:06:08.982 --> 01:06:11.073
<v LATOUR>Mr. Rigmaiden was before my time.

1569
01:06:11.073 --> 01:06:12.119
<v PORTER>Not Mr. Jake.

1570
01:06:12.119 --> 01:06:13.514
You're talking about Josh.

1571
01:06:13.514 --> 01:06:14.560
Mr. Josh Rigmaiden.

1572
01:06:14.560 --> 01:06:17.000
<v HANDY>The one had the grocery store?

1573
01:06:17.000 --> 01:06:18.809
<v JEFFERSON>The one had . . . Josh Rigmaiden.

1574
01:06:18.809 --> 01:06:21.702
He had the grocery store and usually people . . .

1575
01:06:21.702 --> 01:06:23.149
<v JOURDAN>That was Mr. Jake.

1576
01:06:23.149 --> 01:06:24.596
<v PORTER>No that's Mr. Josh.

1577
01:06:24.596 --> 01:06:24.957
<v JOURDAN>Oh.

1578
01:06:24.957 --> 01:06:26.766
<v JEFFERSON>He had the grocery store . . .

1579
01:06:26.766 --> 01:06:27.851
<v LATOUR>[Lola Bell's?] husband?

1580
01:06:27.851 --> 01:06:28.213
<v JEFFERSON>Daddy . . .

1581
01:06:28.213 --> 01:06:28.936
<v HANDY>Her daddy.

1582
01:06:28.936 --> 01:06:29.298
<v JEFFERSON>You . . .

1583
01:06:29.298 --> 01:06:34.000
<v LATOUR>That Mr. Josh was a junior? [Lola?] Bell's husband was a junior?

1584
01:06:34.000 --> 01:06:36.947
<v PORTER>No. We didn't say nothing about junior, baby.

1585
01:06:36.947 --> 01:06:38.053
Mr. Josh Rigmaiden.

1586
01:06:38.053 --> 01:06:41.368
<v LATOUR>I know, but isn't that Lola Bell's husband, Josh?

1587
01:06:41.368 --> 01:06:42.842
<v PORTER>Josh is her daddy.

1588
01:06:42.842 --> 01:06:44.684
<v JEFFERSON>Lola Bell wasn't a Rigmaiden.

1589
01:06:44.684 --> 01:06:47.632
She was a Rigmaiden by her maiden name.

1590
01:06:47.632 --> 01:06:49.474
<v PORTER>She's married to a James.

1591
01:06:49.474 --> 01:06:50.211
<v LATOUR>Oh okay.

1592
01:06:50.211 --> 01:06:52.053
It was Josh James Rigmaiden.

1593
01:06:52.053 --> 01:06:55.000
<v PORTER>Her sister . . . Her son is named Josh.

1594
01:06:55.000 --> 01:07:07.750
<v JEFFERSON>If anybody had a problem or any [person?] they would just go to him and talk about it and that's how we learned . . . That’s how I learned when I was a child that he was one of the leaders in the community.

1595
01:07:07.750 --> 01:07:12.000
<v LATOUR>Oh and they forgot when you was talking about remedies there was a man.

1596
01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:12.304
What

1597
01:07:12.304 --> 01:07:14.125
<c.p>you call him [in the back?]?

1598
01:07:14.125 --> 01:07:14.429
<v JOURDAN>LeDoux.

1599
01:07:14.429 --> 01:07:15.036
<v LATOUR>Mr. LeDoux.

1600
01:07:15.036 --> 01:07:16.250
He would treat people.

1601
01:07:16.250 --> 01:07:17.464
<v ARSENEAULT>He was a treater?

1602
01:07:17.464 --> 01:07:17.768
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1603
01:07:17.768 --> 01:07:20.804
<v PORTER>He’d treat you for worms and all kind of stuff.

1604
01:07:20.804 --> 01:07:22.018
If you had worms . . .

1605
01:07:22.018 --> 01:07:22.625
<v JOURDAN>Toothaches. [. . .?] worms.

1606
01:07:22.625 --> 01:07:23.839
<v HANDY>Don't mention the worms.

1607
01:07:23.839 --> 01:07:25.964
<v JOURDAN>I don’t know nothing about the worms.

1608
01:07:25.964 --> 01:07:28.393
<v HANDY>Look we don't want that in there okay?

1609
01:07:28.393 --> 01:07:34.464
<v PORTER>But then you remember Mr. Ira Garrett that used to have a store across the track when you left Mossville?

1610
01:07:34.464 --> 01:07:37.804
Mr. Ira Garrett, he was a leader too in the church.

1611
01:07:37.804 --> 01:07:39.625
I guess that's . . . in the community.

1612
01:07:39.625 --> 01:07:42.054
I think they were all kind of related.

1613
01:07:42.054 --> 01:07:46.000
<v JEFFERSON>Some of the older people that was here when Mossville was developing.

1614
01:07:46.000 --> 01:07:49.336
<v HANDY>I think a lot of it too was in the church.

1615
01:07:49.336 --> 01:07:53.279
They would take things to church and people . . . Because that's where we assembled.

1616
01:07:53.279 --> 01:07:56.615
And we’d have it at . . . They'd bring it up in church.

1617
01:07:56.615 --> 01:08:05.410
<v JEFFERSON>And our father was instrumental in getting us to ride the school bus because when I was a little girl I'd walk so far and then I'd sat down.

1618
01:08:05.410 --> 01:08:12.082
And I'd tell my brothers and sisters, "If . . . Y'all can't leave me here because my daddy will be really upset with y'all."

1619
01:08:12.082 --> 01:08:14.205
So they would carry me to school.

1620
01:08:14.205 --> 01:08:15.418
And then one day . . .

1621
01:08:15.418 --> 01:08:16.934
<v PORTER>[Pack her on they?] back.

1622
01:08:16.934 --> 01:08:19.664
<v JEFFERSON>My brother told my . . . told him he was tired.

1623
01:08:19.664 --> 01:08:21.180
They say, "Tired of what?"

1624
01:08:21.180 --> 01:08:23.607
He say, "Tired of carrying Kathy to school."

1625
01:08:23.607 --> 01:08:28.459
So he decided to go to the school board and the school board man told him.

1626
01:08:28.459 --> 01:08:31.492
Say, "No it's not a mile from where you live."

1627
01:08:31.492 --> 01:08:33.311
He said, "I tell you what.

1628
01:08:33.311 --> 01:08:37.861
You get in your car and you follow me and we're going to clock this."

1629
01:08:37.861 --> 01:08:41.197
So the school board man got in the car, followed him,

1630
01:08:41.197 --> 01:08:50.598
and they clocked it and my daddy was instrumental in us riding the school bus from Prater Road back when I was in the first or second grade to Mossville School.

1631
01:08:50.598 --> 01:08:51.508
<v JOURDAN>Exactly a mile.

1632
01:08:51.508 --> 01:08:52.418
<v PORTER>Exactly a mile.

1633
01:08:52.418 --> 01:08:55.148
And I was scared of the gully down there.

1634
01:08:55.148 --> 01:08:58.180
And when you get to the gully I had to [ . . .?].

1635
01:08:58.180 --> 01:09:00.000
<v JEFFERSON>She was scared of everything.

1636
01:09:00.000 --> 01:09:02.091
<v LATOUR>When you walk across it, yeah.

1637
01:09:02.091 --> 01:09:08.712
<v HANDY>I didn't say that, but my daddy and brother and I we were five, four, they had a kindergarten.

1638
01:09:08.712 --> 01:09:12.545
The first kindergarten in Mossville and we were part of it.

1639
01:09:12.545 --> 01:09:13.591
<v JEFFERSON>Not the first.

1640
01:09:13.591 --> 01:09:14.636
<v HANDY>Big Mama had . . .

1641
01:09:14.636 --> 01:09:17.424
<v JEFFERSON>No Big Mama was just a substitute teacher.

1642
01:09:17.424 --> 01:09:20.212
<v LATOUR>It was the first kindergarten at the school.

1643
01:09:20.212 --> 01:09:23.000
<v HANDY>Well no, it was at the church.

1644
01:09:23.000 --> 01:09:23.274
<v LATOUR>Okay.

1645
01:09:23.274 --> 01:09:24.643
<v JOURDAN>It wasn’t the first kindergarten.

1646
01:09:24.643 --> 01:09:27.381
<v HANDY>I thought . . . From my perception it was the first kindergarten.

1647
01:09:27.381 --> 01:09:29.298
<v JOURDAN>Because it's the first one you went.

1648
01:09:29.298 --> 01:09:29.845
<v HANDY>That's right.

1649
01:09:29.845 --> 01:09:32.310
Why y'all telling me it's not the first one?

1650
01:09:32.310 --> 01:09:34.226
It's the first one I went to.

1651
01:09:34.226 --> 01:09:36.690
<v JEFFERSON>Well say the first one that you went to.

1652
01:09:36.690 --> 01:09:41.345
<v HANDY>And my daddy was very proud of me graduating from kindergarten and he had to pay that.

1653
01:09:41.345 --> 01:09:42.167
It wasn't free.

1654
01:09:42.167 --> 01:09:44.631
My brother and I he had . . . What was it?

1655
01:09:44.631 --> 01:09:46.000
Fifty cents a week?

1656
01:09:46.000 --> 01:09:47.404
<v JOURDAN>I don't know what it was.

1657
01:09:47.404 --> 01:09:48.106
<v HANDY>Yeah it was . . .

1658
01:09:48.106 --> 01:09:51.383
<v JEFFERSON>It was more than that because we paid a dollar back in the day.

1659
01:09:51.383 --> 01:09:53.021
<v HANDY>Well I thought it was fifty cents.

1660
01:09:53.021 --> 01:09:55.596
<v PORTER>But our grandmother . . . we . . . They had a kindergarten at Mount Zion.

1661
01:09:55.596 --> 01:09:56.532
<v JEFFERSON>Mount Zion Baptist Church.

1662
01:09:56.532 --> 01:09:58.638
<v HANDY>Oh I don't . . . Well that wasn’t in my day.

1663
01:09:58.638 --> 01:10:00.745
<v JEFFERSON>And we would go to school at eight o'clock.

1664
01:10:00.745 --> 01:10:01.915
<v LATOUR>What church yours was at?

1665
01:10:01.915 --> 01:10:04.255
<v JEFFERSON>And at ten o'clock I'd have to catch the bus.

1666
01:10:04.255 --> 01:10:04.957
<v HANDY>Was it [Pasadena?]?

1667
01:10:04.957 --> 01:10:05.426
<v JOURDAN>[Agrees], [Pasadena?].

1668
01:10:05.426 --> 01:10:08.936
<v JEFFERSON>To come back to my grandmother's house because somebody didn't want to go to school.

1669
01:10:08.936 --> 01:10:09.404
<v JOURDAN>Bel Air.

1670
01:10:09.404 --> 01:10:11.511
<v HANDY>In Bel Air Ms. Betty Williams was our teacher.

1671
01:10:11.511 --> 01:10:12.213
<v JOURDAN>I got cut.

1672
01:10:12.213 --> 01:10:12.681
You remember.

1673
01:10:12.681 --> 01:10:13.617
<v JEFFERSON>[Conversation continues in background].

1674
01:10:13.617 --> 01:10:15.021
And I liked going to school,

1675
01:10:15.021 --> 01:10:20.170
but it was over at twelve so I had to bring home at ten the bus didn't pass back until twelve and

1676
01:10:20.170 --> 01:10:20.872
<c.p>school was over.

1677
01:10:20.872 --> 01:10:22.979
And I told my mom I'm tired of this.

1678
01:10:22.979 --> 01:10:24.383
I want to go to school.

1679
01:10:24.383 --> 01:10:27.191
If Karen don't want to go to school let her stay home.

1680
01:10:27.191 --> 01:10:32.106
Then in kindergarten hers was free because my grandfather was paying a dollar and my parents had to pay for mine.

1681
01:10:32.106 --> 01:10:34.447
So she would rob me of my education in kindergarten.

1682
01:10:34.447 --> 01:10:36.319
So they let her drop out of kindergarten.

1683
01:10:36.319 --> 01:10:37.021
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever . . .

1684
01:10:37.021 --> 01:10:37.489
<v JEFFERSON>A what?

1685
01:10:37.489 --> 01:10:38.660
<v ARSENEAULT>A Rosenwald school in Mossville?

1686
01:10:38.660 --> 01:10:40.532
Did y'all ever here about a Rosenwald school?

1687
01:10:40.532 --> 01:10:41.000
<v PORTER>Rosenwald?

1688
01:10:41.000 --> 01:10:42.241
<v ARSENEAULT>An old . . . The old school building.

1689
01:10:42.241 --> 01:10:43.897
<v JEFFERSON>Yeah there was an old school down there . . .

1690
01:10:43.897 --> 01:10:45.966
<v PORTER>The school building was still there when we were kids.

1691
01:10:45.966 --> 01:10:47.621
<v LATOUR>They say that was the first school here.

1692
01:10:47.621 --> 01:10:48.448
<v JEFFERSON>By the recreation center.

1693
01:10:48.448 --> 01:10:49.897
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember what it looked like?

1694
01:10:49.897 --> 01:10:50.310
<v JEFFERSON>It was . . .

1695
01:10:50.310 --> 01:10:53.000
<v PORTER>Yeah I kind of remember, it had a lot of windows and . . .

1696
01:10:53.000 --> 01:10:53.805
<v JEFFERSON>It was white.

1697
01:10:53.805 --> 01:10:54.341
<v JOURDAN>White [agrees].

1698
01:10:54.341 --> 01:10:56.756
<v JEFFERSON>All I remember, white and a lot of windows.

1699
01:10:56.756 --> 01:10:58.366
<v JOURDAN>Had kind of a square front.

1700
01:10:58.366 --> 01:10:59.439
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it located?

1701
01:10:59.439 --> 01:11:00.780
<v JEFFERSON>On Old Spanish Trail close . . .

1702
01:11:00.780 --> 01:11:01.317
<v PORTER>Where the . . .

1703
01:11:01.317 --> 01:11:01.585
<v HANDY>Recreation . . .

1704
01:11:01.585 --> 01:11:04.000
<v PORTER>Right where the recreation and pool and stuff.

1705
01:11:04.000 --> 01:11:05.196
<v LATOUR>I think I vaguely remember.

1706
01:11:05.196 --> 01:11:06.391
<v JOURDAN>Yeah that’s where . . . Along there.

1707
01:11:06.391 --> 01:11:07.348
But they moved it.

1708
01:11:07.348 --> 01:11:09.500
That was the same old buildings that they had.

1709
01:11:09.500 --> 01:11:11.174
You remember white buildings at Mossville School?

1710
01:11:11.174 --> 01:11:11.652
<v LATOUR>At Mossville?

1711
01:11:11.652 --> 01:11:12.848
That was the same buildings?

1712
01:11:12.848 --> 01:11:14.283
<v JOURDAN>That was the same old buildings.

1713
01:11:14.283 --> 01:11:15.000
<v LATOUR>Oh okay.

1714
01:11:15.000 --> 01:11:17.100
<v HANDY>Oh it was kind of by the . . .

1715
01:11:17.100 --> 01:11:18.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all all attending Mossville Elementary?

1716
01:11:18.600 --> 01:11:19.200
<v JEFFERSON>Mossville High.

1717
01:11:19.200 --> 01:11:21.300
<v LATOUR>But we didn't all graduated from there.

1718
01:11:21.300 --> 01:11:22.200
<v JEFFERSON>One through twelve.

1719
01:11:22.200 --> 01:11:24.300
<v LATOUR>Integration we got shipped to Sulphur High.

1720
01:11:24.300 --> 01:11:26.100
<v PORTER>She was the last . . . [indicates Jefferson]

1721
01:11:26.100 --> 01:11:27.600
<v JOURDAN>It was in high school.

1722
01:11:27.600 --> 01:11:29.100
<v PORTER>She was the last class.

1723
01:11:29.100 --> 01:11:29.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1724
01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:30.000
<v JEFFERSON>1969.

1725
01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:33.203
<v PORTER>I was the first one to go to Sulphur High from this area.

1726
01:11:33.203 --> 01:11:33.696
First class.

1727
01:11:33.696 --> 01:11:34.681
<v ARSENEAULT>What was that like?

1728
01:11:34.681 --> 01:11:36.406
<v JOURDAN>That was good about going to school.

1729
01:11:36.406 --> 01:11:37.638
At one time we had . . .

1730
01:11:37.638 --> 01:11:40.348
<v PORTER>We cried, and cried, because it was like fifteen hundred students.

1731
01:11:40.348 --> 01:11:46.507
It was . . . and right when they came down the center road everybody lived on this side of the street had to go to Sulphur High,

1732
01:11:46.507 --> 01:11:49.464
and everybody on that side had to go to Westlake down Prater.

1733
01:11:49.464 --> 01:11:50.696
It was eleven of us.

1734
01:11:50.696 --> 01:11:52.420
The bus would come pick us up.

1735
01:11:52.420 --> 01:11:56.362
<v LATOUR>She cried until she went to Westlake and then she was glad to go to Sulphur.

1736
01:11:56.362 --> 01:11:56.855
<v PORTER>I cried.

1737
01:11:56.855 --> 01:11:58.333
We went to Westlake to school.

1738
01:11:58.333 --> 01:12:00.058
We decided we wasn't going to Sulphur.

1739
01:12:00.058 --> 01:12:01.290
So we go to Westlake.

1740
01:12:01.290 --> 01:12:03.014
<v HANDY>So they say, "Get your own transportation."

1741
01:12:03.014 --> 01:12:03.754
<v PORTER>Your own transportation.

1742
01:12:03.754 --> 01:12:07.449
And we went to Westlake, but then they come in one morning we got to . . .

1743
01:12:07.449 --> 01:12:07.942
<v HANDY>Our mom . . .

1744
01:12:07.942 --> 01:12:13.116
<v PORTER>Our mom took us to school and we got there they rushed us into assembly to get us all . . . not assembly,

1745
01:12:13.116 --> 01:12:16.072
the auditorium because they had people out there with ropes and stuff.

1746
01:12:16.072 --> 01:12:21.000
<v HANDY>Ropes and chains hanging on the trees and they said they were going to kill some of us today.

1747
01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:21.682
<v ARSENEAULT>Nooses and things.

1748
01:12:21.682 --> 01:12:21.909
<v HANDY>Right.

1749
01:12:21.909 --> 01:12:22.136
<v PORTER>Yeah.

1750
01:12:22.136 --> 01:12:24.636
So we . . . Then when that happened we went back to Sulphur.

1751
01:12:24.636 --> 01:12:26.227
We was glad to be in Sulphur.

1752
01:12:26.227 --> 01:12:27.136
<v HANDY>They had a riot.

1753
01:12:27.136 --> 01:12:28.727
<v ARSENEAULT>There was less of that in Sulphur?

1754
01:12:28.727 --> 01:12:31.000
<v LATOUR>Sulphur . . . We didn't have no trouble when we went.

1755
01:12:31.000 --> 01:12:37.043
<v HANDY>When we went back we . . . Mr. Moses wanted to know why we was . . . wanted to go to Westlake so bad.

1756
01:12:37.043 --> 01:12:38.252
And he sit down . . .

1757
01:12:38.252 --> 01:12:40.065
<v LATOUR>He was the principal at Sulphur.

1758
01:12:40.065 --> 01:12:41.273
<v HANDY>He sit down . . . Yeah.

1759
01:12:41.273 --> 01:12:41.878
Billy Moses.

1760
01:12:41.878 --> 01:12:46.410
He sit down and he talked with all of us and he rearranged our schedules.

1761
01:12:46.410 --> 01:12:51.547
He said, "I'm going to make you as comfortable as I can make you at this school.

1762
01:12:51.547 --> 01:12:56.079
So he put . . . We said we didn't like being the only black in the class.

1763
01:12:56.079 --> 01:12:59.705
But yeah there probably wasn't but twenty-five of us at that school.

1764
01:12:59.705 --> 01:13:06.957
So he rearranged schedules so in most of our classes at least we'd have a black . . . Another black student in the classroom with us.

1765
01:13:06.957 --> 01:13:11.791
And we were grateful that we had gone to Sulphur because we didn't have any problems.

1766
01:13:11.791 --> 01:13:13.000
None at all.

1767
01:13:13.000 --> 01:13:14.647
<v PORTER>He didn't allow it . . . The principal.

1768
01:13:14.647 --> 01:13:20.961
<v LATOUR>They say he had an assembly like beforehand and had talked to the kids and told him he wasn't going to tolerate it.

1769
01:13:20.961 --> 01:13:23.706
<v PORTER>He didn't allow . . . And everybody was so nice to us.

1770
01:13:23.706 --> 01:13:23.980
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1771
01:13:23.980 --> 01:13:25.627
<v ARSENEAULT>Why was it different in Westlake?

1772
01:13:25.627 --> 01:13:27.549
<v PORTER>Well it was . . . most of our kids . . .

1773
01:13:27.549 --> 01:13:28.922
<v JOURDAN>More black kids over there.

1774
01:13:28.922 --> 01:13:30.020
I guess they thought . . .

1775
01:13:30.020 --> 01:13:32.765
<v HANDY>From that side on back it was more black children.

1776
01:13:32.765 --> 01:13:35.235
<v PORTER>Going so we wanted to be with our classmates.

1777
01:13:35.235 --> 01:13:37.157
<v JOURDAN>Yeah they had a couple, yeah but . . .

1778
01:13:37.157 --> 01:13:39.078
<v LATOUR>No, she said why was it different.

1779
01:13:39.078 --> 01:13:40.725
I think the demographics was different.

1780
01:13:40.725 --> 01:13:41.000
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1781
01:13:41.000 --> 01:13:42.647
<v LATOUR>The social economic group was different.

1782
01:13:42.647 --> 01:13:42.922
<v HANDY>Right.

1783
01:13:42.922 --> 01:13:43.196
Exactly.

1784
01:13:43.196 --> 01:13:45.941
<v LATOUR>And it just . . . Sulphur just took it better than Westlake.

1785
01:13:45.941 --> 01:13:47.863
<v HANDY>Yeah and that's basically what it was.

1786
01:13:47.863 --> 01:13:49.235
It was a different class.

1787
01:13:49.235 --> 01:13:51.980
<v JOURDAN>I don’t think they had so many blacks going there.

1788
01:13:51.980 --> 01:13:52.529
<v HANDY>Going there.

1789
01:13:52.529 --> 01:13:53.627
It was at Westlake.

1790
01:13:53.627 --> 01:13:55.000
It was a threat.

1791
01:13:55.000 --> 01:13:56.548
<v JOURDAN>They was a threat to people.

1792
01:13:56.548 --> 01:13:57.581
<v HANDY>And then the teachers.

1793
01:13:57.581 --> 01:13:58.355
The teachers from . . .

1794
01:13:58.355 --> 01:14:03.000
<v JOURDAN>But at Sulphur didn't have but a few of us so it wasn't that big of a deal.

1795
01:14:03.000 --> 01:14:06.097
<v HANDY>Then the teachers from Mossville High School also were working at Westlake.

1796
01:14:06.097 --> 01:14:07.645
<v LATOUR>Some of them went to Sulphur.

1797
01:14:07.645 --> 01:14:07.903
<v HANDY>Yeah.

1798
01:14:07.903 --> 01:14:10.742
A few was Sulphur but most of them was at Westlake.

1799
01:14:10.742 --> 01:14:14.097
<v PORTER>But it was much easier on us after we went back to Sulphur.

1800
01:14:14.097 --> 01:14:17.452
<v HANDY>I remember going to a class with Ms. Rogers . . . Everybody know Ms. Rogers.

1801
01:14:17.452 --> 01:14:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was this at Mossville High?

1802
01:14:19.000 --> 01:14:19.894
<v HANDY>No in Westlake.

1803
01:14:19.894 --> 01:14:20.489
We actually . . .

1804
01:14:20.489 --> 01:14:21.681
<v JOURDAN>We’re talking about Mossville.

1805
01:14:21.681 --> 01:14:24.064
We need to get back to Mossville though.

1806
01:14:24.064 --> 01:14:27.936
<v PORTER>Mossville closed the year she graduated [indicates Jefferson] that was the last class.

1807
01:14:27.936 --> 01:14:31.213
And then I was the first class went to . . . the integration.

1808
01:14:31.213 --> 01:14:32.404
We went to Sulphur.

1809
01:14:32.404 --> 01:14:33.298
<v HANDY>The first ones.

1810
01:14:33.298 --> 01:14:34.489
<v PORTER>They went to Maplewood.

1811
01:14:34.489 --> 01:14:36.277
<v HANDY>No I didn't go to Maplewood.

1812
01:14:36.277 --> 01:14:37.766
I went to Sulphur High.

1813
01:14:37.766 --> 01:14:39.851
<v ARSENEAULT>Y'all never went to Mossville High School?

1814
01:14:39.851 --> 01:14:40.745
<v HANDY>Yeah I went.

1815
01:14:40.745 --> 01:14:44.319
<v LATOUR>Back then it was elementary through, but they called it high school.

1816
01:14:44.319 --> 01:14:45.511
But first through twelfth.

1817
01:14:45.511 --> 01:14:46.106
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

1818
01:14:46.106 --> 01:14:47.000
I see.

1819
01:14:47.000 --> 01:14:50.182
<v JOURDAN>At one time it was probably seven of us in school at the same time.

1820
01:14:50.182 --> 01:14:51.667
<v PORTER>Did y'all go to Mossville at all?

1821
01:14:51.667 --> 01:14:51.879
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1822
01:14:51.879 --> 01:14:53.788
<v HANDY>I went to Mossville first grade through ninth grade.

1823
01:14:53.788 --> 01:14:55.697
We integrated to Sulphur High my tenth grade year.

1824
01:14:55.697 --> 01:14:57.606
<v LATOUR>Seventh grade when I switched. [All speaking at once].

1825
01:14:57.606 --> 01:14:59.091
<v PORTER>I was a senior when we went.

1826
01:14:59.091 --> 01:15:01.424
<v JOURDAN>And I was in the eighth . . . I was in the ninth.

1827
01:15:01.424 --> 01:15:02.061
<v LATOUR>I was in . . .

1828
01:15:02.061 --> 01:15:03.545
<v HANDY>You was a senior or a sophomore?

1829
01:15:03.545 --> 01:15:04.394
<v PORTER>I was a senior.

1830
01:15:04.394 --> 01:15:05.455
<v HANDY>Oh I was a sophomore.

1831
01:15:05.455 --> 01:15:06.727
<v LATOUR>I was in the eighth grade.

1832
01:15:06.727 --> 01:15:08.000
I started school at Mossville.

1833
01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:08.775
<v HANDY>We all did.

1834
01:15:08.775 --> 01:15:11.360
<v LATOUR>We had been at school for like a whole week.

1835
01:15:11.360 --> 01:15:13.427
This was like maybe a year after integration.

1836
01:15:13.427 --> 01:15:15.494
We were in school like a whole week.

1837
01:15:15.494 --> 01:15:16.528
We never got books.

1838
01:15:16.528 --> 01:15:22.472
So I told Mama I didn't want to just keep waiting around and she put us in Maplewood if we wanted to go.

1839
01:15:22.472 --> 01:15:25.315
So me and [Gayla?] was the first one to go, huh?

1840
01:15:25.315 --> 01:15:25.831
<v HANDY>To Maplewood?

1841
01:15:25.831 --> 01:15:27.382
<v LATOUR>Then you came the next year?

1842
01:15:27.382 --> 01:15:28.933
<v PORTER>[Or did all of you go?]?

1843
01:15:28.933 --> 01:15:31.000
<v LATOUR>We all went at the same time.

1844
01:15:31.000 --> 01:15:31.864
<v HANDY>The same time.

1845
01:15:31.864 --> 01:15:37.627
<v LATOUR>No because me and [Gayla?] and Jim Milton was the only three blacks at Maplewood when we first went there.

1846
01:15:37.627 --> 01:15:39.356
<v JOURDAN>No we had already went there.

1847
01:15:39.356 --> 01:15:40.508
We was already there.

1848
01:15:40.508 --> 01:15:42.237
<v LATOUR>Yeah but y'all had done left.

1849
01:15:42.237 --> 01:15:43.390
<v JOURDAN>We had done left.

1850
01:15:43.390 --> 01:15:43.678
<v LATOUR>Yeah.

1851
01:15:43.678 --> 01:15:47.424
But the year I went it was just me, [Gayla?], and Jim Milton.

1852
01:15:47.424 --> 01:15:48.000
<v JOURDAN>Yeah.

1853
01:15:48.000 --> 01:15:51.959
<v PORTER>So we really was part of that move, believe it or not.

1854
01:15:51.959 --> 01:15:53.938
<v JOURDAN>Yeah a big part of it.

1855
01:15:53.938 --> 01:15:58.557
<v ARSENEAULT>Is there any particular memory that stands out to you about that time or?

1856
01:15:58.557 --> 01:16:03.505
<v HANDY>My memory that . . . I always say is we didn't want to close Mossville High School.

1857
01:16:03.505 --> 01:16:04.825
We wanted equal books.

1858
01:16:04.825 --> 01:16:06.804
We did not want another school.

1859
01:16:06.804 --> 01:16:09.773
We wanted the same books everybody else was getting.

1860
01:16:09.773 --> 01:16:11.423
<v ARSENEAULT>You were getting used books?

1861
01:16:11.423 --> 01:16:13.072
<v HANDY>We were not . . . Old books.

1862
01:16:13.072 --> 01:16:13.402
<v LATOUR>Old.

1863
01:16:13.402 --> 01:16:13.732
<v JOURDAN>Used.

1864
01:16:13.732 --> 01:16:15.711
<v HANDY>The books that . . . The old editions.

1865
01:16:15.711 --> 01:16:18.021
The other schools had the new editions.

1866
01:16:18.021 --> 01:16:24.289
And the thing that . . . I don't even know why that actually the teachers that we had they loved us.

1867
01:16:24.289 --> 01:16:25.608
They actually loved us.

1868
01:16:25.608 --> 01:16:26.928
<v JOURDAN>Not all of them.

1869
01:16:26.928 --> 01:16:29.237
<v HANDY>You had . . . Did a few things. [laughs]

1870
01:16:29.237 --> 01:16:30.227
<v LATOUR>So bad, Pres.

1871
01:16:30.227 --> 01:16:32.866
You made it hard to love you. [laughs]

1872
01:16:32.866 --> 01:16:36.825
<v HANDY>But anyway they loved us and they wanted us to be educated,

1873
01:16:36.825 --> 01:16:42.763
so they would get material to bring it to school so we would have an equal playing field.

1874
01:16:42.763 --> 01:16:44.742
But that's the thing I remember.

1875
01:16:44.742 --> 01:16:52.000
I didn't care to go to another school I just wanted to have the same material all the other kids had.

1876
01:16:52.000 --> 01:16:53.127
<v PORTER>You know something else, too?

1877
01:16:53.127 --> 01:16:56.732
When we went to Sulphur High it didn't matter what you turned your homework in on.

1878
01:16:56.732 --> 01:16:59.662
You could have done it on brown paper long as you did it.

1879
01:16:59.662 --> 01:17:03.493
But in Mossville we had to have it in order and you had to write correctly and . . .

1880
01:17:03.493 --> 01:17:04.169
<v HANDY>It was different.

1881
01:17:04.169 --> 01:17:05.521
<v PORTER>So it was a difference in . . .

1882
01:17:05.521 --> 01:17:06.197
<v HANDY>And I remember. . .

1883
01:17:06.197 --> 01:17:06.423
<v ARSENEAULT>Standards.

1884
01:17:06.423 --> 01:17:06.648
<v PORTER>Yeah.

1885
01:17:06.648 --> 01:17:08.000
<v JOURDAN>Wasn’t nothing [to turn in.?]

1886
01:17:08.000 --> 01:17:14.429
<v HANDY>I remember when I went to Sulphur High from Mossville Mr. [Arfray?] was the algebra teacher at Mossville High School.

1887
01:17:14.429 --> 01:17:25.357
And he didn't let you sit down from the board until you made that problem even if you had to skip your next class you had to do that problem until you understood it.

1888
01:17:25.357 --> 01:17:29.857
And when we went to Sulphur High my homeroom teacher was an algebra teacher.

1889
01:17:29.857 --> 01:17:37.250
And when we got our first report cards he asked . . . It was my cousin and I were in there because we were J's,

1890
01:17:37.250 --> 01:17:45.286
the Jourdans, and he asked us where did we learn algebra because he couldn't believe we were coming there making A's and B's in algebra.

1891
01:17:45.286 --> 01:17:51.714
So he wanted to know . . . So when he said that I looked him and I said, "At Mossville High School."

1892
01:17:51.714 --> 01:17:52.679
<v JOURDAN>I didn’t get [?]

1893
01:17:52.679 --> 01:17:53.321
<v LATOUR>Me either.

1894
01:17:53.321 --> 01:17:56.536
<v HANDY>And he say, "Oh I didn't realize y'all had algebra."

1895
01:17:56.536 --> 01:17:58.464
<v LATOUR>. . . Maplewood and I didn't learn diddly.

1896
01:17:58.464 --> 01:18:02.000
<v HANDY>So you see what I'm saying, we needed the equal books.

1897
01:18:02.000 --> 01:18:07.464
We didn't need to close our school because when you close a school you close a community.

1898
01:18:07.464 --> 01:18:09.071
The school is the community.

1899
01:18:09.071 --> 01:18:11.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have favorite teachers?

1900
01:18:11.000 --> 01:18:11.462
<v HANDY>Oh yeah.

1901
01:18:11.462 --> 01:18:13.769
Oh you don't want to hear about my favorite teachers.

1902
01:18:13.769 --> 01:18:14.923
<v JEFFERSON>Who was your favorite teacher?

1903
01:18:14.923 --> 01:18:15.615
<v HANDY>All of them.

1904
01:18:15.615 --> 01:18:16.538
<v LATOUR>Ms. Lemelle of course.

1905
01:18:16.538 --> 01:18:19.077
<v HANDY>She came ate biscuits with us, breakfast, and all of them.

1906
01:18:19.077 --> 01:18:19.769
<v PORTER>All our teachers.

1907
01:18:19.769 --> 01:18:20.923
I liked all our teachers.

1908
01:18:20.923 --> 01:18:22.538
<v HANDY>All of our teachers to be honest.

1909
01:18:22.538 --> 01:18:23.692
<v PORTER>You have a favorite, Preston?

1910
01:18:23.692 --> 01:18:24.154
<v JOURDAN>Ms. Lemelle.

1911
01:18:24.154 --> 01:18:25.538
I didn’t like all my teachers.

1912
01:18:25.538 --> 01:18:26.000
<v HANDY>They all . . .

1913
01:18:26.000 --> 01:18:26.462
<v LATOUR>Me either.

1914
01:18:26.462 --> 01:18:27.615
I didn't like Mr. [Reader?].

1915
01:18:27.615 --> 01:18:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her first name?

1916
01:18:29.000 --> 01:18:29.900
<v HANDY>[Josey?] Parker Lemelle.

1917
01:18:29.900 --> 01:18:31.400
I was in her class.

1918
01:18:31.400 --> 01:18:34.400
She graduated from Grambling and that was her first job.

1919
01:18:34.400 --> 01:18:35.600
<v PORTER>I think Ms. Paige . . .

1920
01:18:35.600 --> 01:18:39.500
<v HANDY>But all of our teachers wanted us in their class. [Talking in background].

1921
01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:43.100
All the teachers at Mossville wanted the Jourdan children in their class.

1922
01:18:43.100 --> 01:18:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Why was that?

1923
01:18:44.000 --> 01:18:45.500
<v HANDY>Because we were well disciplined.

1924
01:18:45.500 --> 01:18:51.200
We knew how to do things and we went to school to get an education and not to play.

1925
01:18:51.200 --> 01:18:52.700
<v JOURDAN>You definitely was well disciplined.

1926
01:18:52.700 --> 01:19:00.800
<v HANDY>Because they knew our daddy was going to come if he needed to be there or if he . . . Because he was not there every day with foolishness,

1927
01:19:00.800 --> 01:19:04.100
but when he needed to come to school he was coming.

1928
01:19:04.100 --> 01:19:04.700
And that's . . .

1929
01:19:04.700 --> 01:19:05.600
<v JEFFERSON>Everybody but me.

1930
01:19:05.600 --> 01:19:12.800
I told the teacher, my daddy said back in the days they used to whip, I said my daddy say you can't whip me.

1931
01:19:12.800 --> 01:19:14.600
<v PORTER>I got whippings at school, too.

1932
01:19:14.600 --> 01:19:15.500
<v HANDY>Yeah me too.

1933
01:19:15.500 --> 01:19:18.500
<v JEFFERSON>And the teacher came to my house and he said . . .

1934
01:19:18.500 --> 01:19:20.000
<v HANDY>Which teacher was that?

1935
01:19:20.000 --> 01:19:21.724
<v JEFFERSON>Gayton and Paige and them.

1936
01:19:21.724 --> 01:19:22.414
<v HANDY>Oh yeah.

1937
01:19:22.414 --> 01:19:23.793
I didn't like them.

1938
01:19:23.793 --> 01:19:27.586
<v JEFFERSON>And they said he told them, "Yes you can whip Kathy.

1939
01:19:27.586 --> 01:19:31.379
But she better not come home with no marks on her."

1940
01:19:31.379 --> 01:19:32.759
<v HANDY>And he meant that.

1941
01:19:32.759 --> 01:19:33.103
<v JEFFERSON>So . . .

1942
01:19:33.103 --> 01:19:37.586
<v LATOUR>The only thing I got whippings for was like if I did something . . .

1943
01:19:37.586 --> 01:19:40.000
<v PORTER>I got whippings for math, me.

1944
01:19:40.000 --> 01:19:41.458
<v LATOUR>I got whippings for spelling.

1945
01:19:41.458 --> 01:19:42.917
<v PORTER>With the little peewee stick.

1946
01:19:42.917 --> 01:19:44.375
Y'all never had that whipping?

1947
01:19:44.375 --> 01:19:46.125
<v LATOUR>I always was the one keeping . . .

1948
01:19:46.125 --> 01:19:48.458
<v JEFFERSON>Mr. [Hargrave?] gave you my grade in algebra.

1949
01:19:48.458 --> 01:19:49.333
<v PORTER>You say that.

1950
01:19:49.333 --> 01:19:51.958
You don't know if that's the truth or not.

1951
01:19:51.958 --> 01:19:52.833
<v JEFFERSON>That's the truth.

1952
01:19:52.833 --> 01:19:54.000
<v PORTER>You can't add nothing.

1953
01:19:54.000 --> 01:19:54.875
<v JEFFERSON>Algebra ain't adding.

1954
01:19:54.875 --> 01:20:01.000
<v HANDY>You know what teacher I didn't really like, and I hate to say that, you remember Ms. Valentine from Westlake?

1955
01:20:01.000 --> 01:20:02.489
<v LATOUR>Now you didn't have to say that.

1956
01:20:02.489 --> 01:20:03.128
Strike that off.

1957
01:20:03.128 --> 01:20:03.766
<v PORTER>Strike that off.

1958
01:20:03.766 --> 01:20:05.043
<v HANDY>No, but she was so mean.

1959
01:20:05.043 --> 01:20:05.468
<v JEFFERSON>She was.

1960
01:20:05.468 --> 01:20:06.532
<v HANDY>She was a mean lady.

1961
01:20:06.532 --> 01:20:07.383
<v PORTER>Y'all don't say that.

1962
01:20:07.383 --> 01:20:08.447
<v JOURDAN>Don't say that on there.

1963
01:20:08.447 --> 01:20:09.298
<v HANDY>Oh she was mean.

1964
01:20:09.298 --> 01:20:10.362
I just . . . and I'm not . . .

1965
01:20:10.362 --> 01:20:11.851
<v JOURDAN>You don't want to say that on . . .

1966
01:20:11.851 --> 01:20:12.489
<v PORTER>Strike that off.

1967
01:20:12.489 --> 01:20:14.617
<v HANDY>Oh I don't care if they put . . . It's the truth.

1968
01:20:14.617 --> 01:20:16.319
<v JOURDAN>You don’t want to put that on there.

1969
01:20:16.319 --> 01:20:17.383
<v HANDY>She was a mean teacher.

1970
01:20:17.383 --> 01:20:18.021
<v LATOUR>Strike that off.

1971
01:20:18.021 --> 01:20:21.000
I done took care of her since her old age in home health.

1972
01:20:21.000 --> 01:20:21.943
<v HANDY>Oh she's still living?

1973
01:20:21.943 --> 01:20:23.122
<v LATOUR>She was in Lake Charles.

1974
01:20:23.122 --> 01:20:24.301
<v JEFFERSON>That's probably why she was . . .

1975
01:20:24.301 --> 01:20:25.008
<v ARSENEAULT>What about favorites?

1976
01:20:25.008 --> 01:20:26.894
Ms. Rogers, I know a lot of people . . .

1977
01:20:26.894 --> 01:20:27.837
<v PORTER>Like Ms. Rogers, yeah.

1978
01:20:27.837 --> 01:20:28.780
<v JEFFERSON>I liked Ms. Rogers.

1979
01:20:28.780 --> 01:20:32.317
She was very . . . I wish I'd have just listened to her when she was teaching.

1980
01:20:32.317 --> 01:20:33.732
<v HANDY>She was teaching that English, baby.

1981
01:20:33.732 --> 01:20:35.382
<v PORTER>And I have another teacher Ms. Jones.

1982
01:20:35.382 --> 01:20:36.325
I liked Ms. Jones.

1983
01:20:36.325 --> 01:20:37.976
<v LATOUR>Y'all know y'all liked Ms. Audrey Prater.

1984
01:20:37.976 --> 01:20:38.447
<v PORTER>Ms. Ava.

1985
01:20:38.447 --> 01:20:39.390
<v LATOUR>Oh Ms. Ava Prater.

1986
01:20:39.390 --> 01:20:40.569
<v JEFFERSON>Oh yes Ms. Ava was . . .

1987
01:20:40.569 --> 01:20:44.106
<v HANDY>I liked my first grade teacher and second grade teacher and my third grade teacher.

1988
01:20:44.106 --> 01:20:46.228
<v PORTER>That’s what I say all our teachers were good.

1989
01:20:46.228 --> 01:20:47.642
They all . . . Like they all cared.

1990
01:20:47.642 --> 01:20:48.585
<v HANDY>My fourth grade teacher.

1991
01:20:48.585 --> 01:20:49.293
Ms. [Armstead?] from . . .

1992
01:20:49.293 --> 01:20:50.000
<v JOURDAN>Your favorite?

1993
01:20:50.000 --> 01:20:51.636
<v HANDY>All of those were my favorite.

1994
01:20:51.636 --> 01:20:53.545
I had a lot of favorite teachers.

1995
01:20:53.545 --> 01:20:54.364
<v LATOUR>You know what?

1996
01:20:54.364 --> 01:20:55.182
You know why?

1997
01:20:55.182 --> 01:20:57.909
Because we were never in Ms. Armstead's room . . . or Armstrong.

1998
01:20:57.909 --> 01:20:58.455
That's why.

1999
